00:08:50,510 --> 00:08:52,190
in 2011,

279
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:53,410
they had developed the fifth generation.

280
00:08:53,410 --> 00:08:54,530
They felt it was pure enough

281
00:08:54,530 --> 00:08:56,350
it could be used for teaching purposes,

282
00:08:56,350 --> 00:08:58,010
it could be presented to the world.

283
00:08:58,010 --> 00:09:00,350
So he only did it

284
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:02,390
— of course, as he tells it,

285
00:09:02,390 --> 00:09:03,830
it only took him three months,

286
00:09:03,830 --> 00:09:04,650
basically one summer vacation.

287
00:09:04,650 --> 00:09:06,810
A few teachers from their teaching and research office,

288
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:07,310
along with their students,

289
00:09:07,310 --> 00:09:08,630
managed to build this thing.

290
00:09:08,630 --> 00:09:09,910
But in reality, as we say,

291
00:09:09,910 --> 00:09:11,410
it had actually been developed over many years.

292
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:13,650
It is the crystallization of the knowledge of those who came before.

293
00:09:13,650 --> 00:09:15,510
So,

294
00:09:15,510 --> 00:09:18,310
that's why it's called RISC-V,

295
00:09:18,310 --> 00:09:19,410
and that's where the name comes from.

296
00:09:19,410 --> 00:09:21,310
As for its key people,

297
00:09:21,310 --> 00:09:24,350
these are of course some of the big names of RISC-V.

298
00:09:24,350 --> 00:09:26,270
If any of you are interested later on,

299
00:09:26,270 --> 00:09:27,890
in getting involved in the community,

300
00:09:27,890 --> 00:09:29,710
they are still very active in it.

301
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:30,310
So,

302
00:09:30,310 --> 00:09:37,170
one of them has what looks like an East Asian face,

303
00:09:37,170 --> 00:09:39,730
but I have a feeling he might be Korean.

304
00:09:39,730 --> 00:09:41,290
I don't know the details, I don't know where he's from.

305
00:09:41,290 --> 00:09:44,230
I never looked into it.

306
00:09:44,230 --> 00:09:45,590
But this person is definitely Asian.

307
00:09:45,590 --> 00:09:50,310
The rest are all professors from these universities.

308
00:09:50,310 --> 00:09:53,070
Now, their roles have also changed somewhat.

309
00:09:53,070 --> 00:09:55,710
As we know, RISC-V later established a foundation.

310
00:09:55,710 --> 00:10:03,050
If you're interested, you can go to the official RISC-V website and take a look.

311
00:10:03,050 --> 00:10:03,750
This foundation,

312
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:05,950
this foundation,

313
00:10:05,950 --> 00:10:11,590
the reason we say it can stay open

314
00:10:11,590 --> 00:10:13,010
and free,

315
00:10:13,010 --> 00:10:14,690
the key reason really is that

316
00:10:14,690 --> 00:10:16,930
it is not held in the hands of a single company.

317
00:10:16,930 --> 00:10:18,590
You see what I mean, right?

318
00:10:18,590 --> 00:10:21,210
Once it's in the hands of a single company or enterprise,

319
00:10:21,210 --> 00:10:23,510
as we know, it becomes a matter of money.

320
00:10:23,510 --> 00:10:25,610
And to avoid that problem,

321
00:10:25,610 --> 00:10:27,690
They actually set up a

322
00:10:27,690 --> 00:10:29,930
non-profit foundation

323
00:10:29,930 --> 00:10:31,430
called the RISC-V Foundation.

324
00:10:31,430 --> 00:10:33,230
This is the foundation's website,

325
00:10:33,230 --> 00:10:34,870
so if you're interested, you can take a look.

326
00:10:34,870 --> 00:10:36,230
What they did was

327
00:10:36,230 --> 00:10:38,770
essentially they contributed all of their intellectual property

328
00:10:38,770 --> 00:10:39,990
to the foundation.

329
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:42,170
So it's all open source,

330
00:10:42,170 --> 00:10:45,270
free for everyone to use

331
00:10:45,270 --> 00:10:45,750
and to modify.

332
00:10:45,750 --> 00:10:48,550
And uh,

333
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:50,930
there are no license fees at all.

334
00:10:50,930 --> 00:10:51,830
You can basically do whatever you want with it.

335
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:54,930
Now, uh,

336
00:10:54,930 --> 00:10:56,550
as we just said, it's a set of standards,

337
00:10:56,550 --> 00:10:58,430
so it's not turned into a product.

338
00:10:58,430 --> 00:10:59,030
You see what I mean?

339
00:10:59,030 --> 00:11:01,350
It's not a foundation that makes chips.

340
00:11:01,350 --> 00:11:03,170
It only maintains this set of standards.

341
00:11:03,170 --> 00:11:04,630
This standard is released publicly,

342
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:06,470
so all the vendors,

343
00:11:06,470 --> 00:11:08,850
including individual developers like you, can build on this standard

344
00:11:08,850 --> 00:11:10,150
to develop your own chips.

345
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:12,930
So that is RISC-V's

346
00:11:12,930 --> 00:11:14,670
appeal.

347
00:11:14,670 --> 00:11:18,250
And the instruction architecture it has designed,

348
00:11:18,250 --> 00:11:20,790
you could say it targets

349
00:11:20,790 --> 00:11:22,450
all kinds of application scenarios,

350
00:11:22,450 --> 00:11:23,990
including microcontrollers,

351
00:11:23,990 --> 00:11:26,670
that is, down to the tiny chips in your wearable devices,

352
00:11:26,670 --> 00:11:27,950
or supercomputers—

353
00:11:27,950 --> 00:11:30,170
its design has actually taken all of these into account.

354
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:33,930
So its prospects are very broad.

355
00:11:33,930 --> 00:11:38,150
Over here on the right, I've excerpted

356
00:11:38,150 --> 00:11:42,430
the definition of RISC-V from the RISC-V Foundation's official website.

357
00:11:42,430 --> 00:11:44,010
Actually, everything I just said,

358
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:45,490
it's pretty much the same idea.

359
00:11:45,490 --> 00:11:48,430
You can go read the original text yourselves,

360
00:11:48,430 --> 00:11:50,610
and get a sense of this foundation.

361
00:11:50,610 --> 00:11:53,750
Why did they set up this foundation?

362
00:11:53,750 --> 00:11:57,790
This explains what this whole RISC-V thing is, this concept.

363
00:11:57,790 --> 00:12:02,260
So this RISC-V...

364
00:12:02,260 --> 00:12:07,080
It came out of the university back in 2010.

365
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:09,900
It has been developing up to now, so it's been about ten years or so.

366
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:10,580
Right, ten years.

367
00:12:10,580 --> 00:12:12,080
And over these ten years,

368
00:12:12,080 --> 00:12:14,360
with the establishment of the foundation,

369
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:18,220
including many companies that have also gradually started shifting toward this

370
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:20,240
this, this standard,

371
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:21,300
gradually investing in it.

372
00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:23,520
So this whole RISC-V ecosystem,

373
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,420
its growth has been very rapid.

374
00:12:26,420 --> 00:12:28,160
Now, this is also excerpted

375
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:31,840
from some consulting firms online,

376
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:33,380
their market analysis of it.

377
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:34,020
As you can see,

378
00:12:34,020 --> 00:12:36,920
the market development is still quite attractive.

379
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:40,760
It says that by 2025,

380
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:42,840
they say this market will consume...

381
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:45,300
62.4 billion CPU cores

382
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:47,880
Why is this number so huge?

383
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:49,800
Well, you can probably understand

384
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:52,160
it mainly comes from

385
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:53,620
the consumer market side of things,

386
00:12:53,620 --> 00:12:55,880
where the volume is just enormous.

387
00:12:55,880 --> 00:12:57,980
Take your little phone,

388
00:12:57,980 --> 00:12:59,880
a small router,

389
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:01,340
some tiny device,

390
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:03,260
even a little pair of earphones at home,

391
00:13:03,260 --> 00:13:04,380
and inside there's a small chip.

392
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:06,000
You see what I mean?

393
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,180
So the shipment volume is absolutely massive.

394
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:09,720
That's why he said

395
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:12,040
there will be 62.4 billion of them in 2025.

396
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:13,040
Now, some of you might say,

397
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:14,100
Wow, 62.4 billion? That's so many.

398
00:13:14,100 --> 00:13:15,200
That feels almost impossible.

399
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:16,820
In fact, this number,

400
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:18,660
for the consumer market,

401
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:19,600
It's really not that big a deal

402
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:23,180
So this market segmentation

403
00:13:23,180 --> 00:13:25,760
is used in many industries

404
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:27,220
You can see that its growth

405
00:13:27,220 --> 00:13:28,900
is still very rapid

406
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:34,740
So participating in this,

407
00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:36,460
after this foundation was established,

408
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:37,740
having just a foundation alone

409
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:38,860
really doesn't amount to much

410
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:39,920
What matters mainly is the ecosystem

411
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:41,440
That is to say, worldwide there need to be

412
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:42,920
actual manufacturers

413
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:45,260
and all kinds of players getting involved

414
00:13:45,260 --> 00:13:47,540
Now, through everyone's efforts,

415
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:50,720
the membership of this foundation has grown

416
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:51,900
This is a relatively recent set of figures:

417
00:13:51,900 --> 00:13:54,040
it already exceeds 700

418
00:13:54,040 --> 00:13:55,940
across 50 countries worldwide

419
00:13:55,940 --> 00:13:59,080
And as for China,

420
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:03,860
its participation is actually very strong

421
00:14:03,860 --> 00:14:06,240
We know that our country places a lot of importance on this chip industry,

422
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:08,400
on this whole thing,

423
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:11,240
and ever since some unpleasant things happened,

424
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:12,980
our country has been putting

425
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:15,120
a lot of investment into these choke-point technologies,

426
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:16,480
a very large amount of investment.

427
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:18,480
And chips are one of them.

428
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:21,120
Now, can we just go and buy,

429
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:23,340
that is, buy something like ARM?

430
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:25,280
We know that actually there would be a lot of problems,

431
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:26,400
not just commercial problems,

432
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:31,920
but also that the government of that country would actually have control over it.

433
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:33,780
But with RISC-V,

434
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:35,100
it has one advantage,

435
00:14:35,100 --> 00:14:38,380
which is that it is owned by a foundation,

436
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:43,140
and as you know, that foundation seems to have moved its headquarters to a neutral country,

437
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:43,420
right?

438
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:45,340
Actually, that's precisely to avoid this kind of problem,

439
00:14:45,340 --> 00:14:48,280
that is, to avoid being controlled by a company or a government.

440
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:50,780
So in our view,

441
00:14:50,780 --> 00:14:52,980
That is to say, if we can get involved

442
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:55,780
and help grow it into something big,

443
00:14:55,780 --> 00:14:58,560
then this kind of chokepoint risk in the future

444
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:00,380
I can't say it would be completely gone,

445
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:02,800
but at least it would be much smaller.

446
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:07,040
The key is that you have to be able to get involved.

447
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:09,480
You have to be able to — I mean, it's not just about

448
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:12,320
taking part; you have to take part

449
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:14,720
in particular by getting involved in setting these kinds of standards,

450
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:15,240
and so on.

451
00:15:15,240 --> 00:15:17,200
Only then can you have

452
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:19,380
— that is, only then can you control it, right?

453
00:15:19,380 --> 00:15:20,620
So this,

454
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:26,200
its development status is roughly like this.

455
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:27,200
You can all get familiar with it.

456
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:30,580
Now, next,

457
00:15:30,580 --> 00:15:31,800
everything before this was just some introduction,

458
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:34,560
just a bit of introduction,

459
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:35,720
so you know what it's all about.

460
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:37,100
What RISC-V is like.

461
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:38,080
So next,

462
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:39,140
let's take a closer look

463
00:15:39,140 --> 00:15:40,460
at some things about

464
00:15:40,460 --> 00:15:42,900
this instruction set.

465
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:43,700
First,

466
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:44,920
let me highlight

467
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:45,660
its characteristics for you.

468
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:48,280
So when we talk about the features of RISC-V,

469
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:50,960
this is really also a summary of what came before,

470
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:54,300
and it also sets the stage for what comes next.

471
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:56,880
They can be boiled down to a few points.

472
00:15:56,880 --> 00:15:59,380
First, it's extremely simple.

473
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:03,880
Why do we say the instruction set is designed to be very simple?

474
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:07,380
This was one of the original design goals.

475
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:09,300
Because as you know, RISC-V

476
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:14,160
came along relatively late,

477
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:16,060
so in its overall design,

478
00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:18,720
it actually drew on a lot of the

479
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:21,620
design experience and lessons from earlier instruction sets.

480
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:23,800
Now, those earlier lessons,

481
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:26,840
The typical complaints are that it's too complicated,

482
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:28,460
too massive,

483
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:30,340
hard to use, and so on.

484
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:32,600
So its design is extremely simple.

485
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:36,000
As for the entire specification text,

486
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,020
if you go and read it,

487
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:41,580
you'll find it really doesn't have that many pages in total.

488
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:44,520
Unlike, say, Intel,

489
00:16:44,520 --> 00:16:47,340
or ARM manuals.

490
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:49,940
I don't know if any of you have read them,

491
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:51,480
but if you go out and work in the future,

492
00:16:51,480 --> 00:16:54,300
doing something that deals with this stuff,

493
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:56,820
you'll definitely have to read those kinds of specification manuals.

494
00:16:56,820 --> 00:16:58,420
Now, those specification manuals,

495
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:00,980
like Intel's, run to thousands and thousands of pages.

496
00:17:00,980 --> 00:17:04,040
Reading through them once will cost you your life.

497
00:17:04,040 --> 00:17:07,420
Of course, you have to go over them again and again.

498
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:08,240
You probably can't read them all at once,

499
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:11,680
but this RISC-V specification manual,

500
00:17:11,680 --> 00:17:13,640
it's very small.

501
00:17:13,640 --> 00:17:18,180
we'll talk about that later

502
00:17:18,180 --> 00:17:20,760
that is to say, it's only a few hundred pages in total

503
00:17:20,760 --> 00:17:25,580
so it's designed to be very simple

504
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:28,260
which makes it well suited for teaching

505
00:17:28,260 --> 00:17:30,120
a very good choice for that

506
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:36,120
next, its layering is very clear

507
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:37,420
by clear, I mean

508
00:17:37,420 --> 00:17:40,560
it mainly comes down to the concept of privilege levels inside

509
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,700
we'll cover that in more detail later

510
00:17:42,700 --> 00:17:44,720
the design is layered

511
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:45,520
and then

512
00:17:45,520 --> 00:17:47,360
the third feature is modularity

513
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:48,060
modularity means

514
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:51,640
there is a core instruction set

515
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:53,300
plus some extension instruction sets

516
00:17:53,300 --> 00:17:54,500
that's the approach it uses

517
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:57,480
so where exactly does this modularity show up

518
00:17:57,480 --> 00:17:58,920
we'll cover that later as well

519
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:01,540
the fourth main feature

520
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:02,520
is that it's relatively stable

521
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:03,900
This stability, in my understanding, actually still comes from the fact that

522
00:18:03,900 --> 00:18:05,520
my understanding of it is still that it

523
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:06,480
came out relatively late

524
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:08,160
it drew lessons from the experience of its predecessors

525
00:18:08,160 --> 00:18:09,540
so the design it came up with

526
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:12,460
that doesn't mean that Intel

527
00:18:12,460 --> 00:18:14,280
deliberately wanted to make it this way

528
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:14,780
think about it, right?

529
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:16,040
But why did Intel

530
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:18,500
make privilege level changes so complicated?

531
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:19,620
It's really still for historical reasons,

532
00:18:19,620 --> 00:18:21,520
because a company like Intel,

533
00:18:21,520 --> 00:18:23,800
these old-school processor companies,

534
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:25,460
their entire company's development history

535
00:18:25,460 --> 00:18:26,920
is the history of CPU development.

536
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:28,300
You see what I mean?

537
00:18:28,300 --> 00:18:30,900
It was the first to eat crab — the pioneer,

538
00:18:30,900 --> 00:18:33,140
it waded across the river by feeling the stones.

539
00:18:33,140 --> 00:18:34,440
Now, with RISC-V,

540
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:35,820
the reason it can be stable

541
00:18:35,820 --> 00:18:36,700
Actually, it's still because he

542
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:38,180
took in and absorbed the experience and lessons

543
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:40,020
of his predecessor.

544
00:18:40,020 --> 00:18:41,140
So, in practice, he just

545
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:42,380
once he came out,

546
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:43,880
the things he changed were relatively few.

547
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:44,620
Unlike Intel,

548
00:18:44,620 --> 00:18:45,880
which improves things every year.

549
00:18:45,880 --> 00:18:46,480
Why does it need to improve?

550
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:47,240
Because it's still developing,

551
00:18:47,240 --> 00:18:47,940
right?

552
00:18:47,940 --> 00:18:49,360
Right now, the instruction-level features

553
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:50,420
are actually, relatively speaking,

554
00:18:50,420 --> 00:18:51,760
already relatively stable.

555
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:52,960
And so that,

556
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:53,900
of course, as we said,

557
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:55,180
stability is also one of its advantages.

558
00:18:55,180 --> 00:18:56,940
Now, the fourth point,

559
00:18:56,940 --> 00:18:57,580
is community-driven development.

560
00:18:57,580 --> 00:18:58,060
As for community-driven development,

561
00:18:58,060 --> 00:18:58,480
So it's really about

562
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:00,480
right now it's an open standard

563
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:01,600
that everyone can participate in

564
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:02,060
that kind of thing

565
00:19:02,060 --> 00:19:03,120
rather than a single company

566
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:03,840
calling all the shots

567
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:05,260
That's really

568
00:19:05,260 --> 00:19:07,820
one of the defining features of RISC-V

569
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:11,500
So, more concretely,

570
00:19:11,500 --> 00:19:12,900
let's take a look now

571
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:14,900
First of all, later on in this course,

572
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:18,240
since we're going to study this instruction set standard,

573
00:19:18,240 --> 00:19:21,160
we definitely need to know

574
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:22,680
where to download it from

575
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:24,840
I've already posted the link for you all

576
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:26,040
so you can go and download it

577
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:28,920
Actually, of course, that official document

578
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:30,140
