00:20:24,220 --> 00:20:26,300
So it's a very small booklet.

603
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:29,540
For students who are just getting started,

604
00:20:29,540 --> 00:20:30,980
it might still take some time to read through.

605
00:20:30,980 --> 00:20:32,620
But if you already have a little

606
00:20:32,620 --> 00:20:34,660
development experience in this area,

607
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:36,140
and you go read these two booklets,

608
00:20:36,140 --> 00:20:37,480
you'll find that they're actually

609
00:20:37,480 --> 00:20:39,120
quite easy to get through.

610
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:41,660
Now, these two manuals,

611
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:43,940
we'll be referring back to them often later on,

612
00:20:43,940 --> 00:20:46,100
and showing them to you.

613
00:20:46,100 --> 00:20:47,820
This...

614
00:20:47,820 --> 00:20:53,220
this reflects its simplicity.

615
00:20:53,220 --> 00:20:56,460
So next,

616
00:20:56,460 --> 00:20:59,620
before we start learning the instruction set,

617
00:20:59,620 --> 00:21:00,700
there are some basic concepts

618
00:21:00,700 --> 00:21:01,960
I need to explain to you first.

619
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:02,400
Otherwise,

620
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:03,620
some of the terminology...

621
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:04,120
Actually, that is

622
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:05,660
Otherwise, as we go on later,

623
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:06,940
you might not know what these mean.

624
00:21:06,940 --> 00:21:08,920
First of all,

625
00:21:08,920 --> 00:21:12,440
we should talk about the concept of the instruction set naming format.

626
00:21:12,440 --> 00:21:15,200
Because we'll be seeing these

627
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:16,720
things a lot later on,

628
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:18,420
things like RV32,

629
00:21:18,420 --> 00:21:19,220
RMAR,

630
00:21:19,220 --> 00:21:20,380
RV64GC,

631
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:21,460
what exactly are these?

632
00:21:21,460 --> 00:21:22,520
So I want to explain to you

633
00:21:22,520 --> 00:21:23,340
what this is.

634
00:21:23,340 --> 00:21:24,640
So actually,

635
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:26,040
it is a kind of naming format for it.

636
00:21:26,040 --> 00:21:27,760
Now, this naming format,

637
00:21:27,760 --> 00:21:30,060
we can break it down into a few parts.

638
00:21:30,060 --> 00:21:33,140
Roughly three parts.

639
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:34,180
Let's read it.

640
00:21:34,180 --> 00:21:36,360
The first two, RV

641
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:38,300
Actually, it's a kind of prefix.

642
00:21:38,300 --> 00:21:40,300
The prefix is an abbreviation.

643
00:21:40,300 --> 00:21:41,840
It's the abbreviation of RISC-V.

644
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:44,700
The abbreviation of RISC-V.

645
00:21:44,700 --> 00:21:46,860
You see, RV, we know, stands for RISC-V.

646
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:49,580
And then,

647
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:50,620
after that,

648
00:21:50,620 --> 00:21:51,680
the second part,

649
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:53,260
it is followed by a number.

650
00:21:53,260 --> 00:21:55,060
32, 64, 128.

651
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:58,040
This is the concept of the instruction set's word width that we talked about earlier.

652
00:21:58,040 --> 00:21:59,560
That concept of word width.

653
00:21:59,560 --> 00:22:02,340
We just said this word width,

654
00:22:02,340 --> 00:22:03,520
what does it represent?

655
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:05,160
It's that this processor,

656
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:06,540
its registers,

657
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:08,100
the width of its general-purpose registers,

658
00:22:08,100 --> 00:22:09,220
is 32 bits,

659
00:22:09,220 --> 00:22:10,500
64 bits, or 128 bits.

660
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:13,420
Now, the third part,

661
00:22:13,420 --> 00:22:15,820
is a string of these letters

662
00:22:15,820 --> 00:22:17,500
the string of letters ABCD

663
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:18,840
now, this string of letters

664
00:22:18,840 --> 00:22:19,940
actually has meaning

665
00:22:19,940 --> 00:22:20,900
it actually refers to

666
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:21,760
this

667
00:22:21,760 --> 00:22:24,060
a collection of its modules

668
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:25,580
because as we just mentioned

669
00:22:25,580 --> 00:22:27,380
this RISC-V instruction set

670
00:22:27,380 --> 00:22:28,220
has one feature

671
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:29,660
it is modular

672
00:22:29,660 --> 00:22:30,380
each module

673
00:22:30,380 --> 00:22:31,460
is represented by a letter

674
00:22:31,460 --> 00:22:32,080
to denote it

675
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:33,940
for example, what does I stand for

676
00:22:33,940 --> 00:22:34,800
what does M stand for

677
00:22:34,800 --> 00:22:35,660
what does A stand for

678
00:22:35,660 --> 00:22:37,660
we will cover these later on

679
00:22:37,660 --> 00:22:38,980
so, everyone

680
00:22:38,980 --> 00:22:41,260
when you look at this kind of stuff in the future

681
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:42,460
When it comes to this codename,

682
00:22:42,460 --> 00:22:44,040
you just need to analyze it this way.

683
00:22:44,040 --> 00:22:45,840
So this next example

684
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:47,320
is actually simple for us.

685
00:22:47,320 --> 00:22:48,580
Now we already know

686
00:22:48,580 --> 00:22:49,580
roughly what it means.

687
00:22:49,580 --> 00:22:50,820
Basically, it's like this:

688
00:22:50,820 --> 00:22:54,060
the RISC-V, the 32-bit RISC-V,

689
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:55,760
supports the I module,

690
00:22:55,760 --> 00:22:57,100
the M module, and the A module.

691
00:22:57,100 --> 00:22:58,820
You all get that, right?

692
00:22:58,820 --> 00:22:59,220
In other words,

693
00:22:59,220 --> 00:23:01,520
say you get a certain chip,

694
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:03,000
and since this chip is customized,

695
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:03,700
it can be customized,

696
00:23:03,700 --> 00:23:05,180
then this chip

697
00:23:05,180 --> 00:23:06,940
will definitely tell you in the manual

698
00:23:06,940 --> 00:23:08,040
what kind of support this chip

699
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:11,320
provides in terms of capabilities.

700
00:23:11,320 --> 00:23:12,980
And from that, you can basically tell

701
00:23:12,980 --> 00:23:15,440
that is, it's a 32-bit chip

702
00:23:15,440 --> 00:23:17,900
which features does it support

703
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:19,780
and how many modules is it made up of

704
00:23:19,780 --> 00:23:20,980
you get what I mean

705
00:23:20,980 --> 00:23:24,240
that's what the codename means

706
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:27,800
so the first few parts are fine

707
00:23:27,800 --> 00:23:29,820
but what exactly do these letters and numbers, like IMA, mean

708
00:23:29,820 --> 00:23:31,440
this brings us to

709
00:23:31,520 --> 00:23:33,720
its so-called modular design

710
00:23:33,720 --> 00:23:34,960
as we just said

711
00:23:34,960 --> 00:23:38,040
one design feature of its whole instruction set

712
00:23:38,040 --> 00:23:38,980
is that it's modular

713
00:23:38,980 --> 00:23:41,300
so what does modular mean

714
00:23:41,300 --> 00:23:43,120
well, when it comes to

715
00:23:43,120 --> 00:23:46,580
the development of computer instruction sets in general

716
00:23:46,580 --> 00:23:50,020
there are actually a few typical design approaches

717
00:23:50,020 --> 00:23:52,420
one is called incremental ISA

718
00:23:52,420 --> 00:23:54,840
now, the incremental ISA

719
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:58,840
is our traditional design approach

720
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:00,280
what is incremental ISA

721
00:24:00,280 --> 00:24:04,940
It's really a form of version iteration.

722
00:24:04,940 --> 00:24:06,760
Let me give you an example.

723
00:24:06,760 --> 00:24:09,940
Say I design an instruction set.

724
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:11,000
For instance, the Intel instruction set.

725
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:12,660
Then my first-generation instruction set

726
00:24:12,660 --> 00:24:14,400
might look like this in this diagram.

727
00:24:14,400 --> 00:24:16,960
The early Intel x86 instruction set

728
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:20,280
was designed back in 1978.

729
00:24:20,280 --> 00:24:22,560
At that time, its instruction set was still relatively simple,

730
00:24:22,560 --> 00:24:25,620
because the market demands back then were also quite simple.

731
00:24:25,620 --> 00:24:26,980
It only supported 80 instructions.

732
00:24:26,980 --> 00:24:29,180
Then later on,

733
00:24:29,180 --> 00:24:31,960
for example, when we got to the 1980s,

734
00:24:31,960 --> 00:24:33,200
around 1988 or so,

735
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:34,800
they said our version needed to be upgraded,

736
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:36,900
from version 1.0 to version 2.0.

737
00:24:36,900 --> 00:24:39,020
Then 80 instructions might no longer be enough,

738
00:24:39,020 --> 00:24:41,100
so I might need to expand some instructions.

739
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:42,060
Think about it — isn't that the idea?

740
00:24:42,060 --> 00:24:44,500
And what it actually expanded

741
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:46,580
Just like when we write code,

742
00:24:46,580 --> 00:24:47,780
once this version is upgraded,

743
00:24:47,780 --> 00:24:49,380
there is something we call

744
00:24:49,380 --> 00:24:50,140
Have any of you heard of

745
00:24:50,140 --> 00:24:51,100
this thing called

746
00:24:51,100 --> 00:24:53,480
the concept of compatibility?

747
00:24:53,480 --> 00:24:56,880
Because this second version of mine

748
00:24:56,880 --> 00:24:57,860
cannot just

749
00:24:57,860 --> 00:24:59,380
I can't just say that once I make a second version,

750
00:24:59,380 --> 00:25:00,380
I completely throw away the

751
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:01,320
first version.

752
00:25:01,320 --> 00:25:02,660
That's impossible.

753
00:25:02,660 --> 00:25:03,400
Do you all know why?

754
00:25:03,400 --> 00:25:05,880
Because your customers

755
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:07,860
are not so easy to

756
00:25:07,860 --> 00:25:09,940
just follow along and make changes with you.

757
00:25:09,940 --> 00:25:11,840
For example, the customers of your first version

758
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:12,800
have already gotten used to

759
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:13,580
those 80 instructions

760
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:14,640
when writing programs.

761
00:25:14,640 --> 00:25:16,160
Imagine you suddenly say that a second version comes out

762
00:25:16,160 --> 00:25:17,240
and say those 80 instructions I had before

763
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:18,220
are no longer supported at all

764
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:19,240
and I've come up with a whole new set of instructions

765
00:25:19,240 --> 00:25:20,700
then those customers

766
00:25:20,700 --> 00:25:21,720
would just lose their minds

767
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:22,900
You all get what I'm saying, right?

768
00:25:22,900 --> 00:25:23,980
So what do we do?

769
00:25:23,980 --> 00:25:24,920
I can only say

770
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:25,680
when I'm building a product

771
00:25:25,680 --> 00:25:26,660
it has to work like this

772
00:25:26,660 --> 00:25:27,020
That is to say,

773
00:25:27,020 --> 00:25:28,820
my first version has 80 instructions

774
00:25:28,820 --> 00:25:30,160
and then for my second version,

775
00:25:30,160 --> 00:25:31,840
I add new ones

776
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:33,060
so maybe there are 140 instructions

777
00:25:33,060 --> 00:25:35,640
and when designing those 140 instructions, what's required?

778
00:25:35,640 --> 00:25:39,080
They have to be compatible with the previous 80 instructions

779
00:25:39,080 --> 00:25:40,080
Do you still understand what I mean?

780
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:41,580
By compatible, I mean

781
00:25:41,580 --> 00:25:43,720
all those programs written by the original customers

782
00:25:43,720 --> 00:25:46,400
programs written on the old CPU

783
00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:48,520
still have to be able to run on your new CPU

784
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:51,840
that's the only way the customer will keep playing along with you

785
00:25:51,840 --> 00:25:52,940
Do you understand what I mean?

786
00:25:52,940 --> 00:25:55,940
This approach is called the incremental design approach.

787
00:25:55,940 --> 00:25:57,320
This is also a traditional approach.

788
00:25:57,320 --> 00:25:58,820
An approach that's easy to think of.

789
00:25:58,820 --> 00:26:00,340
So this...

790
00:26:00,340 --> 00:26:02,760
You see this Intel chip here

791
00:26:02,760 --> 00:26:04,320
actually starting from 1978

792
00:26:04,320 --> 00:26:05,040
all the way up to now

793
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:06,000
this instruction set

794
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:07,840
take a look at this picture — it's pretty scary

795
00:26:07,840 --> 00:26:10,260
more than 1300 instructions have already been added

796
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:11,140
because with this...

797
00:26:11,140 --> 00:26:12,120
what do you call it

798
00:26:12,120 --> 00:26:13,640
this design approach of complex instruction sets

799
00:26:13,640 --> 00:26:15,640
you add one instruction

800
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:16,360
add one feature, add one

801
00:26:16,360 --> 00:26:18,160
So the instruction set keeps growing.

802
00:26:18,160 --> 00:26:20,160
But in fact, you'll notice

803
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:23,500
that even its latest CPU instructions

804
00:26:23,500 --> 00:26:26,180
are still compatible with the instructions from 1978.

805
00:26:26,180 --> 00:26:27,480
They are actually still compatible.

806
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:29,500
That's honestly a pretty scary thing.

807
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:30,560
I think you see what I mean.

808
00:26:30,560 --> 00:26:32,880
For a company like Intel,

809
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:33,860
its development teams

810
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:35,060
have a really painful time of it.

811
00:26:35,060 --> 00:26:35,260
Why?

812
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:37,100
Because essentially, you're carrying

813
00:26:37,100 --> 00:26:39,580
that burden and moving forward one step at a time.

814
00:26:39,580 --> 00:26:41,360
Because all these early designs

815
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:43,100
still have to be preserved in the new designs.

816
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:44,320
They have to be kept.

817
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:47,220
So if you go and study

818
00:26:47,220 --> 00:26:49,280
x86,

819
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:52,400
looking at some of that code,

820
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:55,720
you'll find that it preserves a lot of old stuff.

821
00:26:55,720 --> 00:26:56,260
and you don't know.

822
00:26:56,260 --> 00:26:59,440
It can be really hard to figure out why these things are even there.

823
00:26:59,440 --> 00:27:01,520
It's for historical reasons.

824
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:02,740
Because it has to stay compatible,

825
00:27:02,740 --> 00:27:04,040
what we call backward compatibility.

826
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:08,200
This is the traditional approach called incremental design.

827
00:27:08,200 --> 00:27:12,640
Now, as you know, this incremental approach brings about a really bad thing,

828
00:27:12,640 --> 00:27:13,100
and what is that?

829
00:27:13,100 --> 00:27:18,800
The learning cost for people coming after keeps going up,

830
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:21,920
because when you're learning a new instruction set,

831
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:23,280
you still have to take the old instructions

832
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:24,260
that are still there,

833
00:27:24,260 --> 00:27:25,960
you can't just start over.

834
00:27:25,960 --> 00:27:28,760
But as we were saying,

835
00:27:28,760 --> 00:27:33,000
when RISC-V was being designed,

836
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:34,420
it took this problem into account.

837
00:27:34,420 --> 00:27:35,600
Of course, partly because it

838
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:37,240
came along later,

839
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:37,720
so it

840
00:27:37,720 --> 00:27:39,680
doesn't have that historical baggage.

841
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:41,060
You see what I mean, right?

842
00:27:41,060 --> 00:27:43,380
It can be designed entirely from scratch.

843
00:27:43,380 --> 00:27:45,640
It can be designed to be more modular.

844
00:27:45,640 --> 00:27:47,500
It adopts this modular approach.

845
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:49,540
So what do we mean by a modular approach?

846
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:50,140
What it means is,

847
00:27:50,140 --> 00:27:53,560
it only defines one basic instruction set,

848
00:27:53,560 --> 00:27:54,880
one module of the instruction set,

849
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:56,820
called the base integer instruction set.

850
00:27:56,820 --> 00:27:58,540
As for all the other features,

851
00:27:58,540 --> 00:28:01,480
they are added entirely through extensions.

852
00:28:01,480 --> 00:28:03,740
And this base integer instruction set,

853
00:28:03,740 --> 00:28:05,220
remains unchanged.

854
00:28:05,220 --> 00:28:09,440
That is to say, as the spec evolves later on,

855
00:28:09,440 --> 00:28:11,480
its base instruction set stays the same.

856
00:28:11,480 --> 00:28:13,820
So if you want to add new features,

857
00:28:13,820 --> 00:28:16,380
you do it the way you would with plugins.

858
00:28:16,380 --> 00:28:19,260
In software, it's somewhat like this plugin concept.

859
00:28:19,260 --> 00:28:22,340
The main program itself basically doesn't change.

860
00:28:22,340 --> 00:28:24,100
Now, for the people designing the CPU,

861
00:28:24,100 --> 00:28:25,320
So it's relatively

862
00:28:25,320 --> 00:28:28,320
like piling up modules like plugins.

863
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:29,640
So this design,

864
00:28:29,640 --> 00:28:32,140
it's possible that my CPU here,

865
00:28:32,140 --> 00:28:33,500
no matter how things develop,

866
00:28:33,500 --> 00:28:35,360
maybe I won't need those new modules.

867
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:36,340
So my CPU,

868
00:28:36,340 --> 00:28:37,200
that is to say,

869
00:28:37,200 --> 00:28:38,300
ten years from now,

870
00:28:38,300 --> 00:28:39,260
maybe my CPU

871
00:28:39,260 --> 00:28:41,340
still only needs to support 80 instructions.

872
00:28:41,340 --> 00:28:42,220
You see what I mean.

873
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:44,460
Whereas your new CPU

874
00:28:44,460 --> 00:28:46,860
might just stack things on.

875
00:28:46,860 --> 00:28:47,700
So for my new CPU,

876
00:28:47,700 --> 00:28:49,400
adding some modules,

877
00:28:49,400 --> 00:28:51,320
stacking them on like plugins is all it takes.

878
00:28:51,320 --> 00:28:53,800
So that way, it can be relatively

879
00:28:53,800 --> 00:28:56,560
better than a purely incremental approach.

880
00:28:56,560 --> 00:28:59,440
That is the idea of modularity.

881
00:28:59,440 --> 00:29:03,560
So specifically speaking,

882
00:29:03,560 --> 00:29:06,380
the RISC-V instruction set

883
00:29:06,380 --> 00:29:09,340
is made up of

884
00:29:09,340 --> 00:29:11,200
a base integer instruction set

885
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:14,560
plus multiple optional extension instruction sets.

886
00:29:14,560 --> 00:29:18,340
This base integer instruction set,

887
00:29:18,340 --> 00:29:21,380
according to the specification,

888
00:29:21,380 --> 00:29:23,860
is mandatory to implement.

889
00:29:23,860 --> 00:29:24,960
What does mandatory to implement mean?

890
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:27,600
It means no matter what kind of CPU you design,

891
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:31,920
the instructions required by this instruction set

892
00:29:31,920 --> 00:29:33,880
must be implemented.

893
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:35,920
You can't say I only implement the extension parts

894
00:29:35,920 --> 00:29:36,760
without implementing this side.

895
00:29:36,760 --> 00:29:38,240
That won't work.

896
00:29:38,240 --> 00:29:40,740
So this base instruction set

897
00:29:40,740 --> 00:29:42,080
is actually the integer instruction set,

898
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:43,200
the most fundamental stuff for us,

899
00:29:43,200 --> 00:29:45,100
for example, what we'll talk about later,

900
00:29:45,100 --> 00:29:49,100
like addition, subtraction, that kind of stuff, logical operations.

901
00:29:49,100 --> 00:29:49,660
This kind of thing.

902
00:29:49,660 --> 00:29:50,960
These are absolutely required.

903
00:29:50,960 --> 00:29:53,100
Once these instructions are defined, they are locked in for good.

904
00:29:53,100 --> 00:29:54,700
We will never extend them again.

905
00:29:54,700 --> 00:29:59,290
Now, this base instruction set,

906
00:29:59,290 --> 00:30:00,210
strictly speaking,

907
00:30:00,210 --> 00:30:02,850
is actually divided into four kinds.

908
00:30:02,850 --> 00:30:05,210
We already talked about what these code names mean earlier,

909
00:30:05,210 --> 00:30:06,790
and you can probably figure it out yourselves.

910
00:30:06,790 --> 00:30:09,590
Two of them are 32-bit,

911
00:30:09,590 --> 00:30:11,670
one is 64-bit, and one is 128-bit.

912
00:30:11,670 --> 00:30:14,470
Now, as you know, the I here actually stands for what?

913
00:30:14,470 --> 00:30:17,630
We call it the base integer instruction set.

914
00:30:17,630 --> 00:30:19,450
Integer — isn't it integer?

915
00:30:19,450 --> 00:30:19,670
Right?

916
00:30:19,670 --> 00:30:21,810
Its first letter is I, isn't it?

917
00:30:21,810 --> 00:30:22,230
Right?

918
00:30:22,230 --> 00:30:23,490
So when you see this from now on,

919
00:30:23,490 --> 00:30:25,610
32i actually refers to,

920
00:30:25,610 --> 00:30:28,030
the 32-bit base integer instruction set.

921
00:30:28,030 --> 00:30:30,170
So suppose I pick up a CPU and say

922
00:30:30,170 --> 00:30:32,010
it supports RV32i.

923
00:30:32,010 --> 00:30:34,530
That means this CPU only supports what?

924
00:30:34,530 --> 00:30:36,950
The basic integer instruction set.

925
00:30:36,950 --> 00:30:39,830
What about E, then?

926
00:30:39,830 --> 00:30:43,570
E is actually a subset of 32i.

927
00:30:43,570 --> 00:30:47,930
E is actually the abbreviation of the English word 'embed'.

928
00:30:47,930 --> 00:30:51,530
You all should know 'embed', right?

929
00:30:51,530 --> 00:30:54,910
Embed.

930
00:30:54,910 --> 00:30:56,970
Embedded.

931
00:30:56,970 --> 00:30:59,110
What does 'embedded' mean?

932
00:30:59,110 --> 00:31:00,410
Embedded systems, right?

933
00:31:00,410 --> 00:31:01,270
Embedded systems.

934
00:31:01,270 --> 00:31:02,950
E stands for embedded systems.

935
00:31:02,950 --> 00:31:07,190
Now, this E is actually built on the 32-bit i

936
00:31:07,190 --> 00:31:10,030
integer instruction set, with some

937
00:31:10,030 --> 00:31:11,910
optimizations.

938
00:31:11,910 --> 00:31:14,770
For example, it has fewer registers.

939
00:31:14,770 --> 00:31:19,350
It's well suited for making small wearable devices,

940
00:31:19,350 --> 00:31:20,210
embedded devices.

941
00:31:20,210 --> 00:31:24,070
Then there's RV64A and RV128A.

942
00:31:24,070 --> 00:31:25,130
I won't go into those.

943
00:31:25,130 --> 00:31:26,250
Now, one thing to keep in mind at the same time.

944
00:31:26,250 --> 00:31:29,570
That is, there's actually a compatibility aspect here.

945
00:31:29,570 --> 00:31:31,150
There's a small compatibility issue on this side.

946
00:31:31,150 --> 00:31:35,050
That is, the 64-bit version is compatible with the 32-bit one.

947
00:31:35,050 --> 00:31:38,290
The 128-bit version is also compatible with both 64-bit and 32-bit.

948
00:31:38,290 --> 00:31:40,370
So there is a bit of compatibility involved.

949
00:31:40,370 --> 00:31:43,890
What that actually means is,

950
00:31:43,890 --> 00:31:46,390
if what you're building

951
00:31:46,390 --> 00:31:48,650
is a chip that's RV64I,

952
00:31:48,650 --> 00:31:51,550
then something you compiled as 32-bit,

953
00:31:51,550 --> 00:31:53,430
a 32I program,

954
00:31:53,430 --> 00:31:55,010
can still run on the 64-bit version.

955
00:31:55,010 --> 00:31:55,930
I hope you see what I mean here.

956
00:31:55,930 --> 00:31:57,570
That's what this compatibility means.

957
00:31:57,570 --> 00:32:05,360
These are the basic integer instructions.

958
00:32:05,360 --> 00:32:07,700
Now, as for the optional parts, there are quite a few.

959
00:32:07,700 --> 00:32:10,480
By optional modules, I mean there are many of them.

960
00:32:10,480 --> 00:32:12,380
I've just listed a few here.

961
00:32:12,380 --> 00:32:15,380
That is to say, for example, M, A, F.

962
00:32:15,380 --> 00:32:17,180
This M stands for

963
00:32:17,180 --> 00:32:22,380
the instructions that support multiplication and division.

964
00:32:22,380 --> 00:32:25,760
It's short for multiplication.

965
00:32:25,760 --> 00:32:29,200
Then A stands for atomic instructions.

966
00:32:29,200 --> 00:32:30,500
Now these atomic instructions,

967
00:32:30,500 --> 00:32:32,240
basically there are some instructions

968
00:32:32,240 --> 00:32:33,660
that involve the concept of atomic operations.

969
00:32:33,660 --> 00:32:36,780
We should come across some of them later.

970
00:32:36,780 --> 00:32:38,020
We will come across some of them.

971
00:32:38,020 --> 00:32:41,460
Then F is float,

972
00:32:41,460 --> 00:32:44,680
meaning 32-bit floating-point instructions.

973
00:32:44,680 --> 00:32:46,100
D is double.

974
00:32:46,100 --> 00:32:47,860
Those of you who have learned C

975
00:32:47,860 --> 00:32:48,840
should understand this too.

976
00:32:48,840 --> 00:32:50,260
float, double,

977
00:32:50,260 --> 00:32:51,840
the idea is actually very similar.

978
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:55,940
That is, this kind of floating-point computation,

979
00:32:55,940 --> 00:32:57,940
we don't provide it as a base instruction.

980
00:32:57,940 --> 00:32:59,520
It's provided as an extension instruction.

981
00:32:59,520 --> 00:33:00,980
And then there is another one

982
00:33:00,980 --> 00:33:01,920
called compressed instructions.

983
00:33:01,920 --> 00:33:02,780
Now, for compressed instructions,

984
00:33:02,780 --> 00:33:04,980
as we just mentioned,

985
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:06,900
if you don't compress them,

986
00:33:06,900 --> 00:33:07,980
the standard 32

987
00:33:07,980 --> 00:33:09,100
whether it's 32-bit

988
00:33:09,100 --> 00:33:09,860
or 64-bit,

989
00:33:09,860 --> 00:33:11,600
the length of each instruction

990
00:33:11,600 --> 00:33:12,340
as we covered earlier,

991
00:33:12,340 --> 00:33:14,600
is 32 bits in RISC-V.

992
00:33:14,600 --> 00:33:16,160
But as you know,

993
00:33:16,160 --> 00:33:17,480
some embedded systems

994
00:33:17,480 --> 00:33:18,460
have fairly small memory,

995
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:19,860
and they want the program to be a bit smaller.

996
00:33:19,860 --> 00:33:24,020
So we can support what's called a compressed instruction set.

997
00:33:24,020 --> 00:33:26,520
With the compressed instruction set,

998
00:33:26,520 --> 00:33:29,540
each instruction is only half the size of the standard 32-bit one,

999
00:33:29,540 --> 00:33:31,440
so one instruction is 16 bits.

1000
00:33:31,440 --> 00:33:33,340
That makes the program smaller.

1001
00:33:33,340 --> 00:33:33,920
Think about it, isn't that the case?

1002
00:33:33,920 --> 00:33:35,980
So this is what we call compressed

1003
00:33:35,980 --> 00:33:37,640
that's what it means, the compressed instruction set

1004
00:33:37,640 --> 00:33:39,160
Then there are many others

1005
00:33:39,160 --> 00:33:40,200
a lot has been extended

1006
00:33:40,200 --> 00:33:43,740
in this ongoing process of defining the standard

1007
00:33:43,740 --> 00:33:44,980
But no matter how it's defined

1008
00:33:44,980 --> 00:33:47,160
its base instruction set remains unchanged

1009
00:33:47,160 --> 00:33:48,820
So relatively speaking, it's a bit more stable

1010
00:33:48,820 --> 00:33:50,780
Now, for us programmers

1011
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:52,280
that's actually quite friendly

1012
00:33:52,280 --> 00:33:53,620
meaning what you learn today

1013
00:33:53,620 --> 00:33:56,520
might still be usable ten years from now

1014
00:33:56,520 --> 00:33:57,440
You understand what I mean, right?

1015
00:33:57,440 --> 00:34:00,360
Otherwise, it would be painful

1016
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:05,720
So to wrap up, there's a special letter called G

1017
00:34:05,720 --> 00:34:07,200
This G is not a kind of

1018
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:09,840
is not a special instruction set

1019
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:11,140
it's actually a general term

1020
00:34:11,140 --> 00:34:12,680
It actually means

1021
00:34:12,680 --> 00:34:14,380
as specified in its specification,

1022
00:34:14,380 --> 00:34:15,360
this G

1023
00:34:15,360 --> 00:34:18,800
is actually equal to IMFD.

1024
00:34:18,800 --> 00:34:20,240
Now, IMFD, as you know,

1025
00:34:20,240 --> 00:34:21,260
is really just these things:

1026
00:34:21,260 --> 00:34:22,780
it's the integer instruction set —

1027
00:34:22,780 --> 00:34:24,980
it supports integer operations,

1028
00:34:24,980 --> 00:34:26,080
multiplication operations,

1029
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:27,300
atomic operations,

1030
00:34:27,300 --> 00:34:28,260
floating-point operations,

1031
00:34:28,260 --> 00:34:31,020
and double-precision floating-point operations.

1032
00:34:31,020 --> 00:34:32,500
If this CPU

1033
00:34:32,500 --> 00:34:34,560
supports these operations,

1034
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:35,980
then when you write it out, you can make it a bit shorter:

1035
00:34:35,980 --> 00:34:40,950
just use G to stand in for them.

1036
00:34:40,950 --> 00:34:42,010
Now that we understand this,

1037
00:34:42,010 --> 00:34:43,390
when we look again at

1038
00:34:43,390 --> 00:34:46,490
these special symbols,

1039
00:34:46,490 --> 00:34:48,270
there probably shouldn't be any obstacle for you anymore.

1040
00:34:48,270 --> 00:34:50,190
So if later on you see

1041
00:34:50,190 --> 00:34:52,510
The specification has some articles that

1042
00:34:52,510 --> 00:34:54,750
mention things like RV32I.

1043
00:34:54,750 --> 00:34:56,530
Now, think about it yourself and explain what it means.

1044
00:34:56,530 --> 00:34:58,450
Is it just

1045
00:34:58,450 --> 00:35:02,390
a so-called most basic one?

1046
00:35:02,390 --> 00:35:03,810
The RISC-V experiment is really just what?

1047
00:35:03,810 --> 00:35:07,070
It's 32-bit,

1048
00:35:07,070 --> 00:35:08,290
and it only supports what?

1049
00:35:08,290 --> 00:35:10,410
The integer instruction set.

1050
00:35:10,410 --> 00:35:13,510
There are only a few dozen instructions.

1051
00:35:13,510 --> 00:35:14,290
So,

1052
00:35:14,290 --> 00:35:17,870
when we cover instructions later,

1053
00:35:17,870 --> 00:35:19,530
we'll mainly be talking about these integer instructions,

1054
00:35:19,530 --> 00:35:20,490
because these are the basic instructions.

1055
00:35:20,490 --> 00:35:23,190
Once we've mastered the basic instructions,

1056
00:35:23,190 --> 00:35:24,390
there are some extension parts

1057
00:35:24,390 --> 00:35:26,030
that you can read through on your own.

1058
00:35:26,030 --> 00:35:27,490
So,

1059
00:35:27,490 --> 00:35:29,890
RV32IMAC,

1060
00:35:29,890 --> 00:35:31,370
read this one yourself.

1061
00:35:31,370 --> 00:35:33,390
So you can understand this CPU

1062
00:35:33,390 --> 00:35:34,210
If a CPU

1063
00:35:34,210 --> 00:35:35,390
says it supports this,

1064
00:35:35,390 --> 00:35:36,570
then everyone knows

1065
00:35:36,570 --> 00:35:38,110
which instruction features it supports.

1066
00:35:38,110 --> 00:35:39,450
What is this?

1067
00:35:39,450 --> 00:35:41,330
I wrote it down here.

1068
00:35:41,330 --> 00:35:43,890
Then there's also RV64GC.

1069
00:35:43,890 --> 00:35:46,010
You should know that this G needs to be expanded.

1070
00:35:46,010 --> 00:35:47,270
What does G actually stand for?

1071
00:35:47,270 --> 00:35:49,050
IMAFDC.

1072
00:35:49,050 --> 00:35:51,370
Like this C here,

1073
00:35:51,370 --> 00:35:52,090
C is this.

1074
00:35:52,090 --> 00:35:53,730
Actually, G stands for this.

1075
00:35:53,730 --> 00:35:57,670
So this one,

1076
00:35:57,670 --> 00:35:59,650
is its naming convention.

1077
00:35:59,650 --> 00:36:01,950
As a standard being established,

1078
00:36:01,950 --> 00:36:02,970
this is a basic concept.

1079
00:36:02,970 --> 00:36:05,730
So everyone must master it.

1080
00:36:05,730 --> 00:36:09,470
And at the same time, it also reflects this

1081
00:36:09,470 --> 00:36:11,790
This modular concept in RISC-V.

1082
00:36:11,790 --> 00:36:12,450
You can see it from here.

1083
00:36:12,450 --> 00:36:23,440
Now, next,

1084
00:36:23,440 --> 00:36:24,760
the next concept we need to understand

1085
00:36:24,760 --> 00:36:26,960
is the concept of general-purpose registers.

1086
00:36:26,960 --> 00:36:29,960
In this specification,

1087
00:36:29,960 --> 00:36:32,540
when we interact with the processor,

1088
00:36:32,540 --> 00:36:35,620
it's mainly about manipulating these registers,

1089
00:36:35,620 --> 00:36:37,240
like flipping switches,

1090
00:36:37,240 --> 00:36:39,700
to control the processor.

1091
00:36:39,700 --> 00:36:41,340
Now, for this processor,

1092
00:36:41,340 --> 00:36:45,180
the specification defines several kinds of registers.

1093
00:36:45,180 --> 00:36:47,380
One of them is called the general-purpose register,

1094
00:36:47,380 --> 00:36:48,580
which is also the most fundamental kind.

1095
00:36:48,580 --> 00:36:49,920
For these registers,

1096
00:36:49,920 --> 00:36:52,100
there are 32 defined in total.

1097
00:36:52,100 --> 00:36:54,100
32 defined in total.

1098
00:36:54,100 --> 00:36:55,420
If you look over on the right side here,

1099
00:36:55,420 --> 00:36:57,660
in this standard specification,

1100
00:36:57,660 --> 00:36:59,300
each one has a code name.

1101
00:36:59,300 --> 00:37:01,780
One set is called x0 all the way to x31.

1102
00:37:01,780 --> 00:37:03,740
So that's 32 registers, right?

1103
00:37:03,740 --> 00:37:06,260
Now these 32 registers,

1104
00:37:06,260 --> 00:37:11,240
whether it's a 32-bit CPU,

1105
00:37:11,240 --> 00:37:13,260
64-bit, or 128-bit, they're all the same.

1106
00:37:13,260 --> 00:37:16,660
If you want to support floating-point operations,

1107
00:37:16,660 --> 00:37:18,780
then there are separate floating-point registers to use.

1108
00:37:18,780 --> 00:37:21,920
We won't cover those here for now.

1109
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:23,500
Our focus is still on learning these,

1110
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:26,780
the most basic general-purpose registers — the 32 general-purpose registers.

1111
00:37:26,780 --> 00:37:30,920
Now, 32E — we talked about this earlier.

1112
00:37:30,920 --> 00:37:32,480
Now, everyone pay attention here.

1113
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:36,180
In this diagram, from the specification,

1114
00:37:36,180 --> 00:37:37,720
from the standard — that is, my reference 1,

1115
00:37:37,720 --> 00:37:38,940
the reference 1 written earlier,

1116
00:37:38,940 --> 00:37:42,440
it's from section 2.1 of the unprivileged instruction document.

1117
00:37:42,440 --> 00:37:43,720
This diagram was extracted from there.

1118
00:37:43,720 --> 00:37:47,960
In this diagram, besides the 32 general-purpose registers,

1119
00:37:47,960 --> 00:37:52,000
it also draws a register called the PC register.

1120
00:37:52,000 --> 00:37:53,220
As we know, this PC,

1121
00:37:53,220 --> 00:37:57,060
If you've studied assembly, or studied it on X86,

1122
00:37:57,060 --> 00:37:59,740
we know that PC is the program counter

1123
00:37:59,740 --> 00:38:01,540
— the register that holds the program pointer, right?

1124
00:38:01,540 --> 00:38:04,720
On some other instruction architectures,

1125
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:07,640
we know that this PC register is exposed.

1126
00:38:07,640 --> 00:38:08,360
What do I mean by exposed?

1127
00:38:08,360 --> 00:38:11,400
I mean that by writing assembly instructions,

1128
00:38:11,400 --> 00:38:13,020
you can manipulate this register,

1129
00:38:13,020 --> 00:38:14,460
reading it or writing it.

1130
00:38:14,460 --> 00:38:17,160
Have any of you done that?

1131
00:38:17,160 --> 00:38:18,680
For example, when debugging a program,

1132
00:38:18,680 --> 00:38:21,680
you might even modify the PC register,

1133
00:38:21,680 --> 00:38:23,180
to make your program jump somewhere else.

1134
00:38:23,180 --> 00:38:23,920
Have you ever done anything like that?

1135
00:38:23,920 --> 00:38:26,520
Maybe you haven't run into that situation yet.

1136
00:38:26,520 --> 00:38:29,120
But sometimes when we debug programs,

1137
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:30,060
we use little tricks like that.

1138
00:38:30,060 --> 00:38:32,600
However, in RISC-V, note that

1139
00:38:32,600 --> 00:38:36,040
the PC register is not exposed.

1140
00:38:36,040 --> 00:38:37,420
Even though it's drawn here,

1141
00:38:37,420 --> 00:38:38,860
Actually, when you're programming,

1142
00:38:38,860 --> 00:38:40,500
remember that we can't access

1143
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:41,600
this PC register.

1144
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:42,860
Of course, this thing definitely exists,

1145
00:38:42,860 --> 00:38:43,760
because without it,

1146
00:38:43,760 --> 00:38:45,360
the CPU simply couldn't run.

1147
00:38:45,360 --> 00:38:46,820
When I demonstrated this to you last class,

1148
00:38:46,820 --> 00:38:47,460
you saw it, right?

1149
00:38:47,460 --> 00:38:49,480
So then, why doesn't RISC-V

1150
00:38:49,480 --> 00:38:51,200
expose the PC register

1151
00:38:51,200 --> 00:38:52,120
for you to access?

1152
00:38:52,120 --> 00:38:53,880
It's mainly because of certain

1153
00:38:53,880 --> 00:38:57,040
issues in modern CPU design,

1154
00:38:57,040 --> 00:38:59,440
specifically pipeline design issues.

1155
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:02,860
That's more of an advanced topic,

1156
00:39:02,860 --> 00:39:04,820
so we won't cover it here.

1157
00:39:04,820 --> 00:39:06,320
But the main thing to know is that

1158
00:39:06,320 --> 00:39:07,080
this PC register

1159
00:39:07,080 --> 00:39:08,020
is something we cannot access,

1160
00:39:08,020 --> 00:39:09,080
but it does exist.

1161
00:39:09,080 --> 00:39:10,980
If you really want to get

1162
00:39:10,980 --> 00:39:12,260
the value of this PC register,

1163
00:39:12,260 --> 00:39:13,960
it is possible to go and read it,

1164
00:39:13,960 --> 00:39:14,680
but when you read it,

1165
00:39:14,680 --> 00:39:16,360
it is read in another special way.

1166
00:39:16,360 --> 00:39:17,420
Here,

1167
00:39:17,420 --> 00:39:19,240
there may be some examples later,

1168
00:39:19,240 --> 00:39:20,700
and I'll introduce them to you.

1169
00:39:20,700 --> 00:39:24,000
So that's one issue.

1170
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:26,200
As for the rest,

1171
00:39:26,200 --> 00:39:28,460
as for the rest,

1172
00:39:28,460 --> 00:39:30,640
well, regarding registers,

1173
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:31,600
that is, the general-purpose registers,

1174
00:39:31,600 --> 00:39:34,580
I'll stop the introduction here.

1175
00:39:34,580 --> 00:39:38,280
The next concept,

1176
00:39:38,280 --> 00:39:41,620
once we finish this part,

1177
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:42,800
this second lecture will be

1178
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:43,820
a bit longer,

1179
00:39:43,820 --> 00:39:46,600
and we'll cover the material on Risk5

1180
00:39:46,600 --> 00:39:48,960
as a whole.

1181
00:39:48,960 --> 00:39:51,560
So the next one is,

1182
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:53,280
these are actually all basic concepts

1183
00:39:53,280 --> 00:39:54,520
extracted from the spec.

1184
00:39:54,520 --> 00:39:56,800
If you want to read the spec in the future,

1185
00:39:56,800 --> 00:39:59,580
hopefully this will help you

1186
00:39:59,580 --> 00:40:00,640
when you read it later on.

1187
00:40:00,640 --> 00:40:03,860
Now, in this RISC-V spec,

1188
00:40:03,860 --> 00:40:04,620
it also mentions a concept

1189
00:40:04,620 --> 00:40:05,520
called the hart concept.

1190
00:40:05,520 --> 00:40:08,800
You'll often see this term mentioned in various places.

1191
00:40:08,800 --> 00:40:10,260
So what is a hart?

1192
00:40:10,260 --> 00:40:11,520
Well, this word is actually

1193
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:13,600
a term coined within

1194
00:40:13,600 --> 00:40:14,600
the RISC-V spec itself.

1195
00:40:14,600 --> 00:40:15,600
In the English language,

1196
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:16,520
you won't find this word in a dictionary.

1197
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:19,200
It's not a real English word.

1198
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:20,560
It actually stands for

1199
00:40:20,560 --> 00:40:22,380
what we wrote later on,

1200
00:40:22,380 --> 00:40:24,360
a Hardware Thread,

1201
00:40:24,360 --> 00:40:27,000
It takes the first three letters of Hardware

1202
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:28,420
and the first letter of Thread

1203
00:40:28,420 --> 00:40:29,360
and puts them together into this word.

1204
00:40:29,360 --> 00:40:32,000
Now, translated into Chinese,

1205
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:34,480
it's called the hardware thread concept.

1206
00:40:34,480 --> 00:40:36,380
And this concept is very important.

1207
00:40:36,380 --> 00:40:37,580
Why?

1208
00:40:37,580 --> 00:40:39,280
What do we mean by hardware thread?

1209
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:42,100
Let me explain that to you.

1210
00:40:42,100 --> 00:40:44,060
Now, as you know,

1211
00:40:44,060 --> 00:40:46,180
on our processors,

1212
00:40:46,180 --> 00:40:47,340
in the early days,

1213
00:40:47,340 --> 00:40:48,860
it was generally the processor concept,

1214
00:40:48,860 --> 00:40:50,280
the CPU, right?

1215
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:52,720
Of course, in some documents,

1216
00:40:52,720 --> 00:40:54,260
you'll also see the concept of a core,

1217
00:40:54,260 --> 00:40:56,500
the concept of a CPU core.

1218
00:40:56,500 --> 00:40:59,060
But they all really refer to the concept of the CPU.

1219
00:40:59,060 --> 00:41:00,300
Now, when we say CPU,

1220
00:41:00,300 --> 00:41:02,240
in the early days of the CPU,

1221
00:41:02,240 --> 00:41:03,700
Inside a CPU,

1222
00:41:03,700 --> 00:41:06,120
it can only execute instructions one at a time, sequentially.

1223
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:07,820
As for the CPU's execution process,

1224
00:41:07,820 --> 00:41:09,580
we demonstrated it to you in the last class.

1225
00:41:09,580 --> 00:41:11,080
You remember, right?

1226
00:41:11,080 --> 00:41:12,680
There's a CU unit, the control unit.

1227
00:41:12,680 --> 00:41:13,860
Instruction by instruction, what is this process called?

1228
00:41:13,860 --> 00:41:14,700
First, fetch.

1229
00:41:14,700 --> 00:41:16,140
The first step is called fetch.

1230
00:41:16,140 --> 00:41:16,980
What's the second step called?

1231
00:41:16,980 --> 00:41:19,700
It's called decode, right?

1232
00:41:19,700 --> 00:41:21,440
Then the third step is execute.

1233
00:41:21,440 --> 00:41:23,200
It does this over and over again.

1234
00:41:23,200 --> 00:41:24,280
It's like a wheel spinning there.

1235
00:41:24,280 --> 00:41:26,320
So since it keeps repeating this,

1236
00:41:26,320 --> 00:41:28,540
you can actually imagine it as,

1237
00:41:28,540 --> 00:41:29,500
essentially a stream of instructions,

1238
00:41:29,500 --> 00:41:30,060
right?

1239
00:41:30,060 --> 00:41:31,760
executing one after another.

1240
00:41:31,760 --> 00:41:33,900
So it becomes — there's only one CPU in there.

1241
00:41:33,900 --> 00:41:35,960
So generally, if there's only one CU, or control unit,

1242
00:41:35,960 --> 00:41:39,160
there's only one instruction stream being executed in there, right?

1243
00:41:39,160 --> 00:41:39,900
Isn't that the case?

1244
00:41:39,900 --> 00:41:44,040
That's exactly how early CPUs used to work.

1245
00:41:44,040 --> 00:41:48,020
But as hardware has developed,

1246
00:41:48,020 --> 00:41:49,960
you get a situation like this:

1247
00:41:49,960 --> 00:41:52,420
I buy a machine that comes with just one CPU,

1248
00:41:52,420 --> 00:41:54,640
but inside that CPU,

1249
00:41:54,640 --> 00:41:55,700
the spec sheet will tell you

1250
00:41:55,700 --> 00:41:57,680
there are two execution streams in there.

1251
00:41:57,680 --> 00:42:00,000
Has anyone heard of this concept?

1252
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:02,240
On Intel chips, for instance,

1253
00:42:02,240 --> 00:42:03,580
there's this thing called hyper-threading.

1254
00:42:03,580 --> 00:42:04,160
You've heard of it?

1255
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:07,160
The concept of Hyper-Threading.

1256
00:42:07,160 --> 00:42:07,920
Right?

1257
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:09,040
So if you open up

1258
00:42:09,040 --> 00:42:10,960
the Task Manager,

1259
00:42:10,960 --> 00:42:11,840
say you bought one

1260
00:42:11,840 --> 00:42:14,740
that's advertised as 8 cores

1261
00:42:14,740 --> 00:42:16,420
or a 16-core

1262
00:42:16,420 --> 00:42:18,000
CPU when it comes back,

1263
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:18,880
but if you open

1264
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:20,880
the Task Manager

1265
00:42:20,880 --> 00:42:21,800
and once you open it,

1266
00:42:21,800 --> 00:42:22,360
if you take a look,

1267
00:42:22,360 --> 00:42:23,680
the actual

1268
00:42:23,680 --> 00:42:24,820
hardware processors inside

1269
00:42:24,820 --> 00:42:26,340
are actually halved.

1270
00:42:26,340 --> 00:42:26,800
That is to say,

1271
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:28,140
Intel's design approach

1272
00:42:28,140 --> 00:42:28,760
means that

1273
00:42:28,760 --> 00:42:30,100
within what it claims as one core,

1274
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:31,220
it can run at the same time

1275
00:42:31,220 --> 00:42:32,020
two execution streams.

1276
00:42:32,020 --> 00:42:34,100
This is one of its hardware designs.

1277
00:42:34,100 --> 00:42:36,840
This is a concept on modern processors.

1278
00:42:36,840 --> 00:42:40,440
Now, once this thing was introduced,

1279
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:43,480
it led to a conceptual confusion for us,

1280
00:42:43,480 --> 00:42:45,180
which is that before, when we talked about one core,

1281
00:42:45,180 --> 00:42:47,020
There's only one execution stream inside a core.

1282
00:42:47,020 --> 00:42:48,480
And then there's no room for confusion.

1283
00:42:48,480 --> 00:42:50,360
But now, another concept has been introduced.

1284
00:42:50,360 --> 00:42:51,660
Namely, having two execution streams inside one core.

1285
00:42:51,660 --> 00:42:52,940
That does cause confusion.

1286
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:53,380
Think about it, doesn't it?

1287
00:42:53,380 --> 00:42:55,840
So he understands what I mean by this.

1288
00:42:55,840 --> 00:42:59,680
So when RISC-V was defining its specification,

1289
00:42:59,680 --> 00:43:00,920
precisely to avoid this confusion,

1290
00:43:00,920 --> 00:43:02,420
because using the terms core or CPU

1291
00:43:02,420 --> 00:43:03,940
can easily mix up these concepts,

1292
00:43:03,940 --> 00:43:06,480
they introduced a new abstract concept

1293
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:07,160
called a hart.

1294
00:43:07,160 --> 00:43:11,860
That is, they took the concept of an execution stream

1295
00:43:11,860 --> 00:43:13,740
and fully abstracted it into something called a hart.

1296
00:43:13,740 --> 00:43:14,720
You can think of it as just a

1297
00:43:14,720 --> 00:43:17,160
one hart is essentially a virtual CPU.

1298
00:43:17,160 --> 00:43:18,440
He understands.

1299
00:43:18,440 --> 00:43:20,880
But in reality, inside an actual processor,

1300
00:43:20,880 --> 00:43:21,700
there may be two harts.

1301
00:43:21,700 --> 00:43:23,520
This is possible.

1302
00:43:23,520 --> 00:43:25,100
And each hart here,

1303
00:43:25,100 --> 00:43:26,660
is an independent execution concept.

1304
00:43:26,660 --> 00:43:31,340
That is, this passage is taken from the specification.

1305
00:43:31,340 --> 00:43:32,760
Please go read this passage.

1306
00:43:32,760 --> 00:43:35,920
What concept is he emphasizing?

1307
00:43:35,920 --> 00:43:41,120
He says, from the perspective of software

1308
00:43:41,120 --> 00:43:44,140
running in a given execution environment,

1309
00:43:44,140 --> 00:43:47,700
that is, from the perspective of software programming,

1310
00:43:47,700 --> 00:43:49,200
what is a hart?

1311
00:43:49,200 --> 00:43:51,920
A hart is a resource.

1312
00:43:51,920 --> 00:43:53,460
This is a very abstract concept,

1313
00:43:53,460 --> 00:43:54,000
isn't it?

1314
00:43:54,000 --> 00:43:56,680
He doesn't say it's a CPU or anything like that.

1315
00:43:56,680 --> 00:43:58,120
It's just a kind of resource.

1316
00:43:58,120 --> 00:43:59,200
This thing is an object,

1317
00:43:59,200 --> 00:44:00,080
and what is it?

1318
00:44:00,080 --> 00:44:06,160
It can autonomously fetch and execute instructions.

1319
00:44:06,160 --> 00:44:08,260
That is, this object,

1320
00:44:08,260 --> 00:44:11,760
what does it mean that it can autonomously do so?

1321
00:44:11,760 --> 00:44:14,440
It expresses this sense of autonomy,

1322
00:44:14,440 --> 00:44:16,540
an independent concept, free from interference.

1323
00:44:16,540 --> 00:44:17,480
Do you follow what I mean?

1324
00:44:17,480 --> 00:44:20,360
In other words, it's an abstract resource,

1325
00:44:20,360 --> 00:44:23,000
a resource that can independently fetch

1326
00:44:23,000 --> 00:44:24,480
We covered this concept in class.

1327
00:44:24,480 --> 00:44:25,760
Fetch means fetching instructions,

1328
00:44:25,760 --> 00:44:26,540
right?

1329
00:44:26,540 --> 00:44:27,980
And execute

1330
00:44:27,980 --> 00:44:30,640
go fetch and execute these instructions.

1331
00:44:30,640 --> 00:44:32,440
Isn't this

1332
00:44:32,440 --> 00:44:33,980
what we just talked about,

1333
00:44:33,980 --> 00:44:35,320
this execution stream?

1334
00:44:35,320 --> 00:44:36,560
These are two independent execution streams.

1335
00:44:36,560 --> 00:44:37,420
Do you follow what I mean here?

1336
00:44:37,420 --> 00:44:39,480
Just picture it as

1337
00:44:39,480 --> 00:44:41,960
two independent cores.

1338
00:44:41,960 --> 00:44:43,580
Of course, it's called a hart,

1339
00:44:43,580 --> 00:44:44,140
not a core.

1340
00:44:44,140 --> 00:44:44,660
It's called a hart.

1341
00:44:44,660 --> 00:44:45,780
Just imagine it

1342
00:44:45,780 --> 00:44:46,880
as the things we'll talk about later

1343
00:44:46,880 --> 00:44:47,420
actually a hard...

1344
00:44:47,420 --> 00:44:48,000
you can imagine it

1345
00:44:48,000 --> 00:44:49,920
as a CPU running independently

1346
00:44:49,920 --> 00:44:52,560
It represents an independent, running stream of instructions

1347
00:44:52,560 --> 00:44:54,120
Like that

1348
00:44:54,120 --> 00:44:56,100
maybe that makes it a bit easier to understand

1349
00:44:56,100 --> 00:44:59,960
So this is the concept of a hart introduced in the specification

1350
00:44:59,960 --> 00:45:00,700
This concept is very important

1351
00:45:00,700 --> 00:45:01,580
it comes up everywhere

1352
00:45:01,580 --> 00:45:05,720
If you confuse it with what we usually call a CPU or processor,

1353
00:45:05,720 --> 00:45:07,480
it may cause you some trouble

1354
00:45:07,480 --> 00:45:08,620
So here

1355
00:45:08,620 --> 00:45:10,580
I want to emphasize it

1356
00:45:10,580 --> 00:45:12,700
Now that we have this concept of a hart,

1357
00:45:12,700 --> 00:45:15,420
we won't use the term processor from now on

1358
00:45:15,420 --> 00:45:16,200
And what I mean is,

1359
00:45:16,200 --> 00:45:18,480
in all the specifications,

1360
00:45:18,480 --> 00:45:19,740
they only use the term hart.

1361
00:45:19,740 --> 00:45:21,340
without mentioning the concept of a processor,

1362
00:45:21,340 --> 00:45:23,720
because it's possible that the processor we get back,

1363
00:45:23,720 --> 00:45:25,340
might have several execution streams

1364
00:45:25,340 --> 00:45:26,080
running at the same time.

1365
00:45:26,080 --> 00:45:28,020
So to avoid this problem,

1366
00:45:28,020 --> 00:45:29,740
ok,

1367
00:45:29,740 --> 00:45:30,980
this is the concept of hart,

1368
00:45:30,980 --> 00:45:35,600
and it's what we want you to grasp.

1369
00:45:35,600 --> 00:45:36,660
Ok.

1370
00:45:36,660 --> 00:45:36,940
Now,

1371
00:45:36,940 --> 00:45:40,580
there's another fairly important concept

1372
00:45:40,580 --> 00:45:41,800
called privilege level.

1373
00:45:41,800 --> 00:45:43,640
This is also something we mentioned just now.

1374
00:45:43,640 --> 00:45:44,500
We said RISC-V,

1375
00:45:44,500 --> 00:45:45,300
its specification,

1376
00:45:45,300 --> 00:45:46,840
it defines a layered concept.

1377
00:45:46,840 --> 00:45:47,920
This concept,

1378
00:45:47,920 --> 00:45:48,760
well, the layered concept,

1379
00:45:48,760 --> 00:45:49,520
is actually relatively

1380
00:45:49,520 --> 00:45:50,680
easy to understand.

1381
00:45:50,680 --> 00:45:51,640
That is to say,

1382
00:45:51,640 --> 00:45:54,140
what is this thing similar to?

1383
00:45:54,140 --> 00:45:55,120
Those of you who have studied Linux,

1384
00:45:55,120 --> 00:45:56,700
we know that in Linux

1385
00:45:56,700 --> 00:45:57,960
there's often this thing,

1386
00:45:57,960 --> 00:45:59,120
and it's been taught before,

1387
00:45:59,120 --> 00:46:00,340
called kernel mode and user mode.

1388
00:46:00,340 --> 00:46:01,220
Do you understand it?

1389
00:46:01,220 --> 00:46:02,880
Did a teacher ever cover this in class?

1390
00:46:02,880 --> 00:46:04,700
User mode and kernel mode,

1391
00:46:04,700 --> 00:46:05,520
what exactly is the concept?

1392
00:46:05,520 --> 00:46:08,380
Did anyone tie it to the hardware

1393
00:46:08,380 --> 00:46:09,520
and explain this concept to you?

1394
00:46:09,520 --> 00:46:10,880
That would make it easier to understand

1395
00:46:10,880 --> 00:46:11,980
what the concept of user mode and kernel mode

1396
00:46:11,980 --> 00:46:12,820
really is.

1397
00:46:12,820 --> 00:46:15,480
Now, in RISC-V,

1398
00:46:15,480 --> 00:46:16,260
these privilege levels,

1399
00:46:16,260 --> 00:46:17,360
they're actually related to that user mode

1400
00:46:17,360 --> 00:46:18,740
and kernel mode.

1401
00:46:18,740 --> 00:46:19,960
It's not that they're somewhat related.

1402
00:46:19,960 --> 00:46:21,140
They are very strongly related.

1403
00:46:21,140 --> 00:46:22,700
So, we say,

1404
00:46:22,700 --> 00:46:24,020
in hardware,

1405
00:46:24,020 --> 00:46:26,400
what it means is that when you design a CPU,

1406
00:46:26,400 --> 00:46:27,960
you have to provide three levels.

1407
00:46:27,960 --> 00:46:29,900
You can think of it as a switch.

1408
00:46:29,900 --> 00:46:31,760
Flip it one way and you're in user mode.

1409
00:46:31,760 --> 00:46:33,540
Flip it the other way and it's called

1410
00:46:33,540 --> 00:46:34,620
the three states it defines,

1411
00:46:34,620 --> 00:46:36,280
called user mode,

1412
00:46:36,280 --> 00:46:38,220
supervisor mode, and machine mode.

1413
00:46:38,220 --> 00:46:41,180
So, flip the switch one way and it's user mode.

1414
00:46:41,180 --> 00:46:43,360
Put the switch in the middle and it's supervisor mode.

1415
00:46:43,360 --> 00:46:45,220
Flip it further down and it's machine mode.

1416
00:46:45,220 --> 00:46:46,340
You can understand it as something like that.

1417
00:46:46,340 --> 00:46:47,960
So,

1418
00:46:47,960 --> 00:46:51,380
to give an analogy,

1419
00:46:51,380 --> 00:46:54,620
for a system like Linux, an operating system like ours,

1420
00:46:54,620 --> 00:46:57,600
when you run in what we call user mode,

1421
00:46:57,600 --> 00:47:01,120
which actually tells us that our CPU is running in the user mode on top of this

1422
00:47:01,120 --> 00:47:03,120
the switch is set to user mode

1423
00:47:03,120 --> 00:47:07,120
and when we enter the kernel, which mode do we run in?

1424
00:47:07,120 --> 00:47:10,160
actually, we run in supervisor mode

1425
00:47:10,160 --> 00:47:13,980
machine mode is actually a higher level than supervisor mode

1426
00:47:13,980 --> 00:47:17,420
in terms of privilege, from top to bottom

1427
00:47:17,420 --> 00:47:20,940
the level gets stronger and higher

1428
00:47:20,940 --> 00:47:25,540
so this, uh, machine mode, you can understand it as

1429
00:47:26,940 --> 00:47:27,540
uh

1430
00:47:27,940 --> 00:47:29,140
it's called machine mode, right

1431
00:47:29,140 --> 00:47:31,540
it's called machine mode. Uh, what is machine mode exactly?

1432
00:47:31,940 --> 00:47:32,440
uh

1433
00:47:33,340 --> 00:47:34,740
if any of you have studied, uh...

1434
00:47:34,740 --> 00:47:37,240
because some of you may have seen that x86 standard

1435
00:47:37,240 --> 00:47:38,740
x86, haven't you...

1436
00:47:39,140 --> 00:47:39,540
you've...

1437
00:47:40,740 --> 00:47:42,140
I mean, you've come across this concept

1438
00:47:42,140 --> 00:47:44,540
called real mode and protected mode

1439
00:47:45,540 --> 00:47:46,940
there's also this concept

1440
00:47:47,440 --> 00:47:50,140
basically, what it means is that we...

1441
00:47:50,140 --> 00:47:51,620
This Intel chip,

1442
00:47:51,620 --> 00:47:53,620
during boot, works like this.

1443
00:47:53,620 --> 00:47:54,500
Once it powers on,

1444
00:47:54,500 --> 00:47:56,180
the first thing it enters is real mode.

1445
00:47:56,180 --> 00:47:57,720
What is real mode?

1446
00:47:57,720 --> 00:47:58,760
When it's in real mode,

1447
00:47:58,760 --> 00:48:00,980
the virtual address doesn't take effect.

1448
00:48:00,980 --> 00:48:01,840
We've talked about this concept before.

1449
00:48:01,840 --> 00:48:04,020
When you were learning Linux,

1450
00:48:04,020 --> 00:48:06,720
regarding that virtual address,

1451
00:48:06,720 --> 00:48:07,380
when we talked about that kind of concept,

1452
00:48:07,380 --> 00:48:09,300
we covered a similar idea.

1453
00:48:09,300 --> 00:48:12,300
That is, Intel's chips,

1454
00:48:12,300 --> 00:48:13,020
as soon as they power on,

1455
00:48:13,020 --> 00:48:14,880
actually enter what's called real mode.

1456
00:48:14,880 --> 00:48:16,120
In real mode,

1457
00:48:16,120 --> 00:48:17,720
virtual addresses don't work.

1458
00:48:17,720 --> 00:48:19,040
The addresses we access at that point,

1459
00:48:19,040 --> 00:48:20,300
they're all physical addresses.

1460
00:48:20,300 --> 00:48:21,540
Remember this concept?

1461
00:48:21,540 --> 00:48:25,120
Then when the program runs to a certain state,

1462
00:48:25,120 --> 00:48:28,080
after you modify one of the CPU's registers,

1463
00:48:28,080 --> 00:48:29,940
the CPU jumps to what's called

1464
00:48:29,940 --> 00:48:32,160
protected mode.

1465
00:48:32,160 --> 00:48:33,780
Once you're in protected mode,

1466
00:48:33,780 --> 00:48:36,640
there's this thing called the MMU that gets turned on.

1467
00:48:36,640 --> 00:48:38,640
You've probably heard that term before, right?

1468
00:48:38,640 --> 00:48:41,120
If you've forgotten, go back and search for it.

1469
00:48:41,120 --> 00:48:43,200
I'm just using it as an example.

1470
00:48:43,200 --> 00:48:45,300
Actually, RISC-V is very similar to this concept.

1471
00:48:45,300 --> 00:48:47,960
That is, when you first power on,

1472
00:48:47,960 --> 00:48:50,260
our RISC-V processor,

1473
00:48:50,260 --> 00:48:52,000
it runs in machine mode,

1474
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:54,100
which is the most core mode.

1475
00:48:54,100 --> 00:48:56,260
This mode is mandatory to implement.

1476
00:48:56,260 --> 00:48:58,660
It's somewhat similar to x86's

1477
00:48:58,660 --> 00:48:59,800
real mode,

1478
00:48:59,800 --> 00:49:01,960
it's just that people use different names for it.

1479
00:49:01,960 --> 00:49:04,300
And when running in machine mode,

1480
00:49:04,300 --> 00:49:06,220
virtual addresses are not enabled.

1481
00:49:06,220 --> 00:49:08,280
everything runs in physical addresses

1482
00:49:08,280 --> 00:49:10,040
in the code you access,

1483
00:49:10,040 --> 00:49:11,120
the addresses written there

1484
00:49:11,120 --> 00:49:13,440
are all the actual corresponding physical addresses.

1485
00:49:13,440 --> 00:49:14,660
Then,

1486
00:49:14,660 --> 00:49:16,440
there is a way to

1487
00:49:16,440 --> 00:49:18,340
jump to supervisor type.

1488
00:49:18,340 --> 00:49:20,200
After it's in supervisor type,

1489
00:49:20,200 --> 00:49:22,620
it's essentially like entering protected mode.

1490
00:49:22,620 --> 00:49:25,860
Then virtual addresses are enabled,

1491
00:49:25,860 --> 00:49:27,640
and at that point the addresses it accesses

1492
00:49:27,640 --> 00:49:29,900
are all handled as virtual addresses.

1493
00:49:29,900 --> 00:49:31,060
Now as for user type,

1494
00:49:31,060 --> 00:49:32,540
it's actually somewhat similar to what

1495
00:49:32,540 --> 00:49:36,520
we talked about in Linux,

1496
00:49:36,520 --> 00:49:38,080
which is user mode.

1497
00:49:38,080 --> 00:49:40,760
So these three modes it defines

1498
00:49:40,760 --> 00:49:43,080
actually serve a layered protection role,

1499
00:49:43,080 --> 00:49:44,600
which is quite similar to the concept

1500
00:49:44,600 --> 00:49:45,580
found in other chips.

1501
00:49:45,580 --> 00:49:48,440
So this is the concept of

1502
00:49:48,440 --> 00:49:49,700
privilege levels in RISC-V.

1503
00:49:49,700 --> 00:49:52,980
Now, besides these three levels,

1504
00:49:52,980 --> 00:49:54,680
there is also one called Debug mode.

1505
00:49:54,680 --> 00:49:55,840
What Debug mode means is

1506
00:49:55,840 --> 00:49:57,220
it is used specifically for debugging the CPU.

1507
00:49:57,220 --> 00:49:59,620
This level has even higher privileges,

1508
00:49:59,620 --> 00:50:01,640
but we don't use it in ordinary programming.

1509
00:50:01,640 --> 00:50:03,440
So I'll just mention it here and leave it at that.

1510
00:50:03,440 --> 00:50:08,740
Now, why do we bring up these three modes?

1511
00:50:08,740 --> 00:50:09,920
The point is this:

1512
00:50:09,920 --> 00:50:12,900
later on, when you look at a chip,

1513
00:50:12,900 --> 00:50:13,740
it will often say

1514
00:50:13,740 --> 00:50:14,580
this chip,

1515
00:50:14,580 --> 00:50:18,140
for example, this chip only supports M-mode.

1516
00:50:18,140 --> 00:50:18,940
What does that mean?

1517
00:50:18,940 --> 00:50:21,760
It means that when you program on this chip,

1518
00:50:21,760 --> 00:50:23,320
you can only access physical addresses.

1519
00:50:23,320 --> 00:50:24,940
You cannot enable virtual addresses,

1520
00:50:24,940 --> 00:50:28,300
so you can't do processing using virtual addresses.

1521
00:50:28,300 --> 00:50:29,460
He understood what I meant.

1522
00:50:29,460 --> 00:50:32,960
Then what if it only supports M and U?

1523
00:50:32,960 --> 00:50:34,040
That is to say, it does not support

1524
00:50:34,040 --> 00:50:36,600
and likewise, it also does not support the MMU.

1525
00:50:36,600 --> 00:50:38,540
But it splits out one level

1526
00:50:38,540 --> 00:50:40,480
M and U.

1527
00:50:40,480 --> 00:50:43,220
Then the most complete one

1528
00:50:43,220 --> 00:50:44,980
supports all three of these levels.

1529
00:50:44,980 --> 00:50:45,820
So in our

1530
00:50:45,820 --> 00:50:47,300
for example, a general-purpose

1531
00:50:47,300 --> 00:50:49,120
chip inside a computer,

1532
00:50:49,120 --> 00:50:51,320
it definitely has to support all three modes: M, S, and U.

1533
00:50:51,320 --> 00:50:53,900
Because only after the MMU is turned on

1534
00:50:53,900 --> 00:50:55,220
and virtual addresses are supported,

1535
00:50:55,220 --> 00:50:58,480
can we implement something like the concept of a process.

1536
00:50:58,480 --> 00:51:00,040
And what I mean is,

1537
00:51:00,040 --> 00:51:01,500
the concept of a process

1538
00:51:01,500 --> 00:51:03,720
can only be implemented

1539
00:51:03,720 --> 00:51:04,760
after virtual addresses are enabled.

1540
00:51:04,760 --> 00:51:06,780
Otherwise, it simply cannot be done.

1541
00:51:06,780 --> 00:51:07,900
Of course,

1542
00:51:07,900 --> 00:51:10,340
in order to implement this virtual address,

1543
00:51:10,340 --> 00:51:13,200
you need hardware support like a special MMU,

1544
00:51:13,200 --> 00:51:16,780
so the demands on the CPU design will be a bit higher.

1545
00:51:16,780 --> 00:51:19,120
For the operating system example

1546
00:51:19,120 --> 00:51:20,280
we'll be doing for you later,

1547
00:51:20,280 --> 00:51:21,760
since we have limited time,

1548
00:51:21,760 --> 00:51:23,420
we won't get into virtual addresses.

1549
00:51:23,420 --> 00:51:24,400
So we,

1550
00:51:24,400 --> 00:51:26,700
in practice, we'll

1551
00:51:26,700 --> 00:51:31,760
only work in this mode,

1552
00:51:31,760 --> 00:51:35,080
that is, we support M-mode and U-mode,

1553
00:51:35,080 --> 00:51:36,960
two privilege levels.

1554
00:51:36,960 --> 00:51:39,940
So at least you'll get a feel for

1555
00:51:39,940 --> 00:51:40,860
this user mode and,

1556
00:51:40,860 --> 00:51:43,220
it's somewhat similar to kernel mode,

1557
00:51:43,220 --> 00:51:44,760
because M is also a kind of kernel mode.

1558
00:51:44,760 --> 00:51:45,460
Think about it, isn't it?

1559
00:51:45,460 --> 00:51:47,860
It's also operating in a kernel working mode.

1560
00:51:47,860 --> 00:51:49,520
So we say

1561
00:51:49,520 --> 00:51:52,800
Why can it provide this kind of tiered protection?

1562
00:51:52,800 --> 00:51:54,460
The key, actually, lies in

1563
00:51:54,460 --> 00:51:56,020
the concept we're going to talk about next,

1564
00:51:56,020 --> 00:51:58,780
which is that at different privilege levels,

1565
00:51:58,780 --> 00:52:00,820
there is its own set of registers.

1566
00:52:00,820 --> 00:52:02,680
That is what truly achieves

1567
00:52:02,680 --> 00:52:05,000
the effect of our tiered protection.

1568
00:52:05,000 --> 00:52:06,960
In other words, it's not just about flipping a switch over,

1569
00:52:06,960 --> 00:52:07,780
saying we flip it over,

1570
00:52:07,780 --> 00:52:08,900
flip it to user mode,

1571
00:52:08,900 --> 00:52:10,940
or flip it to machine type,

1572
00:52:10,940 --> 00:52:11,860
and that's it.

1573
00:52:11,860 --> 00:52:12,280
Not at all.

1574
00:52:12,280 --> 00:52:13,080
The real reason is that,

1575
00:52:13,080 --> 00:52:15,100
from the CPU's point of view,

1576
00:52:15,100 --> 00:52:16,740
at different privilege levels,

1577
00:52:16,740 --> 00:52:18,520
it has its own independent set of registers,

1578
00:52:18,520 --> 00:52:20,260
and these different registers,

1579
00:52:20,260 --> 00:52:21,300
in different modes,

1580
00:52:21,300 --> 00:52:22,740
cannot be accessed across each other.

1581
00:52:22,740 --> 00:52:24,640
For example, if I now take the CPU

1582
00:52:24,640 --> 00:52:25,740
and flip that switch,

1583
00:52:25,740 --> 00:52:27,160
switch it over to user mode,

1584
00:52:27,160 --> 00:52:29,600
then those registers in M mode

1585
00:52:29,600 --> 00:52:31,800
and the registers in supervisor mode

1586
00:52:31,800 --> 00:52:33,240
it may no longer be able to access them.

1587
00:52:33,240 --> 00:52:35,380
Doesn't that give us a protection effect?

1588
00:52:35,380 --> 00:52:36,440
Do you follow what I mean?

1589
00:52:36,440 --> 00:52:38,720
This is what we call kernel mode and user mode.

1590
00:52:38,720 --> 00:52:40,460
Actually, the most core concept is right here.

1591
00:52:40,460 --> 00:52:43,900
Now, this set of registers,

1592
00:52:43,900 --> 00:52:44,960
we call them

1593
00:52:44,960 --> 00:52:47,680
Control Under Status Registers,

1594
00:52:47,680 --> 00:52:48,520
that is, they're called

1595
00:52:48,520 --> 00:52:49,540
or for short, CSR.

1596
00:52:49,540 --> 00:52:50,580
Later on,

1597
00:52:50,580 --> 00:52:53,540
since we'll be writing an operating system,

1598
00:52:53,540 --> 00:52:55,760
we'll be dealing with CSRs a lot.

1599
00:52:55,760 --> 00:52:57,120
If you're only writing

1600
00:52:57,120 --> 00:52:58,440
ordinary user-space programs,

1601
00:52:58,440 --> 00:52:59,640
In fact, in your code,

1602
00:52:59,640 --> 00:53:01,880
there won't be any CSR register operations.

1603
00:53:01,880 --> 00:53:05,240
But since we need to work with the kernel,

1604
00:53:05,240 --> 00:53:06,980
when we start writing the kernel,

1605
00:53:06,980 --> 00:53:08,180
we will come across this stuff.

1606
00:53:08,180 --> 00:53:11,260
So I hope everyone takes the time to really understand this concept.

1607
00:53:11,260 --> 00:53:13,520
Now, here,

1608
00:53:13,520 --> 00:53:16,040
as we just mentioned,

1609
00:53:16,040 --> 00:53:17,080
at different levels,

1610
00:53:17,080 --> 00:53:18,520
under different privilege levels,

1611
00:53:18,520 --> 00:53:20,920
each one has its own corresponding set of registers.

1612
00:53:20,920 --> 00:53:22,060
That's what I just said,

1613
00:53:22,060 --> 00:53:25,540
the U/S/M modes each have their own registers.

1614
00:53:25,540 --> 00:53:28,360
So here I've briefly listed them out for you.

1615
00:53:28,360 --> 00:53:32,720
This actually refers to the supervisor mode,

1616
00:53:32,720 --> 00:53:34,580
which has its own independent set of registers.

1617
00:53:34,580 --> 00:53:36,460
And correspondingly, in machine mode,

1618
00:53:36,460 --> 00:53:37,240
and in user mode,

1619
00:53:37,240 --> 00:53:38,440
there are also their own registers.

1620
00:53:38,440 --> 00:53:39,620
But since space is limited here,

1621
00:53:39,620 --> 00:53:40,300
I won't list them all here.

1622
00:53:40,300 --> 00:53:41,800
You can go and look them up yourselves.

1623
00:53:41,800 --> 00:53:43,120
Reference 2 is

1624
00:53:43,120 --> 00:53:45,380
the spec we just mentioned.

1625
00:53:45,380 --> 00:53:46,260
Isn't it split into two parts?

1626
00:53:46,260 --> 00:53:46,960
Split into two volumes?

1627
00:53:46,960 --> 00:53:47,880
There's the upper volume and the lower volume.

1628
00:53:47,880 --> 00:53:50,480
The lower volume is the one about privilege levels.

1629
00:53:50,480 --> 00:53:52,360
In this spec,

1630
00:53:52,360 --> 00:53:54,540
there's an introduction to the registers

1631
00:53:54,540 --> 00:53:55,860
under each privilege level.

1632
00:53:55,860 --> 00:53:56,680
Go and take a look.

1633
00:53:56,680 --> 00:54:00,480
Each level has its own set,

1634
00:54:00,480 --> 00:54:01,400
so this achieves

1635
00:54:01,400 --> 00:54:02,360
the protective effect.

1636
00:54:02,360 --> 00:54:03,340
At the same time,

1637
00:54:03,340 --> 00:54:04,700
you can see that

1638
00:54:04,700 --> 00:54:06,280
a higher privilege level

1639
00:54:06,280 --> 00:54:07,320
can access a lower one,

1640
00:54:07,320 --> 00:54:08,820
but not the other way around.

1641
00:54:08,820 --> 00:54:11,020
That is to say, when your CPU

1642
00:54:11,020 --> 00:54:12,640
is working in, say, M-mode,

1643
00:54:12,640 --> 00:54:14,300
and M-mode is the highest privilege level,

1644
00:54:14,300 --> 00:54:16,840
then when you're working in M-mode,

1645
00:54:16,840 --> 00:54:18,060
you can access

1646
00:54:18,060 --> 00:54:19,820
the registers of the lower privilege levels.

1647
00:54:19,820 --> 00:54:22,780
That's the concept of privilege.

1648
00:54:22,780 --> 00:54:23,620
See what I mean?

1649
00:54:23,620 --> 00:54:24,900
That's pretty easy to understand, right?

1650
00:54:24,900 --> 00:54:26,180
But the reverse does not work.

1651
00:54:26,180 --> 00:54:27,620
So for a program running in user mode,

1652
00:54:27,620 --> 00:54:29,620
if you try to force it inside a user-mode program,

1653
00:54:29,620 --> 00:54:31,720
by writing an instruction that writes

1654
00:54:31,720 --> 00:54:34,860
or reads a register from a higher privilege level,

1655
00:54:34,860 --> 00:54:36,220
it will cause an exception.

1656
00:54:36,280 --> 00:54:37,500
That is, an exception will be raised,

1657
00:54:37,500 --> 00:54:38,540
and your program will crash.

1658
00:54:38,540 --> 00:54:40,860
This is what provides the protection.

1659
00:54:40,860 --> 00:54:46,910
Now correspondingly, when it comes to operating the CSRs,

1660
00:54:46,910 --> 00:54:48,150
those are another set of registers.

1661
00:54:48,150 --> 00:54:50,210
So for operating on this set of registers,

1662
00:54:50,210 --> 00:54:51,250
there are dedicated instructions.

1663
00:54:51,250 --> 00:54:53,890
These instructions are also provided through extended instruction sets,

1664
00:54:53,890 --> 00:54:56,530
that is, through the concept of modularization.

1665
00:54:56,530 --> 00:55:00,880
Now, between different instruction levels,

1666
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:02,160
you can switch.

1667
00:55:02,160 --> 00:55:05,040
What does this instruction switching mean?

1668
00:55:05,040 --> 00:55:06,080
If you all

1669
00:55:06,080 --> 00:55:07,780
actually, once you've learned that,

1670
00:55:07,780 --> 00:55:09,440
you know how system calls are implemented.

1671
00:55:09,440 --> 00:55:11,380
What is a system call?

1672
00:55:11,380 --> 00:55:14,480
A system call is when your user-mode program,

1673
00:55:14,480 --> 00:55:15,980
the application running in user mode,

1674
00:55:15,980 --> 00:55:17,840
needs to access something in kernel mode,

1675
00:55:17,840 --> 00:55:18,120
right?

1676
00:55:18,120 --> 00:55:20,620
Doesn't that involve a mode switch?

1677
00:55:20,620 --> 00:55:22,680
So there are dedicated instructions

1678
00:55:22,680 --> 00:55:24,980
that help us switch from one mode to another,

1679
00:55:24,980 --> 00:55:27,540
or here, between different privilege levels.

1680
00:55:27,540 --> 00:55:30,520
Now, the e-call instruction and the e-break instruction

1681
00:55:30,520 --> 00:55:30,940
That's exactly it.

1682
00:55:30,940 --> 00:55:33,220
Later on, when we get to system calls,

1683
00:55:33,220 --> 00:55:36,280
we'll walk you through that implementation process.

1684
00:55:36,280 --> 00:55:41,060
So that's the concept of privilege levels

1685
00:55:41,060 --> 00:55:43,400
and CSRs.

1686
00:55:43,400 --> 00:55:44,420
This is also something

1687
00:55:44,420 --> 00:55:45,880
we want everyone to master — a key point.

1688
00:55:45,880 --> 00:55:49,570
Now we have the last two parts left,

1689
00:55:49,570 --> 00:55:51,370
and once we finish them,

1690
00:55:51,370 --> 00:55:52,590
we'll take a break.

1691
00:55:52,590 --> 00:55:54,610
One of those parts

1692
00:55:54,610 --> 00:55:55,530
is memory protection.

1693
00:55:55,530 --> 00:55:57,270
Memory management and protection.

1694
00:55:57,270 --> 00:55:59,030
As we just mentioned,

1695
00:55:59,030 --> 00:56:00,390
our RISC-V,

1696
00:56:00,390 --> 00:56:02,190
the RISC-V instruction set specification,

1697
00:56:02,190 --> 00:56:05,350
doesn't just define instructions.

1698
00:56:05,350 --> 00:56:06,670
It also defines a lot of other things.

1699
00:56:06,670 --> 00:56:08,070
Among them, memory management and protection

1700
00:56:08,070 --> 00:56:10,770
is also part of its specification.

1701
00:56:10,770 --> 00:56:12,710
Now, as for this memory protection,

1702
00:56:12,710 --> 00:56:15,270
in RISC-V it's divided into two kinds.

1703
00:56:15,270 --> 00:56:16,810
One is called physical memory protection,

1704
00:56:16,810 --> 00:56:17,890
and the other is called virtual memory.

1705
00:56:17,890 --> 00:56:20,730
So basically, physical memory protection

1706
00:56:20,730 --> 00:56:23,910
is a low-level memory management approach.

1707
00:56:23,910 --> 00:56:24,910
Virtual memory,

1708
00:56:24,910 --> 00:56:27,150
you can think of it as a high-level memory management approach.

1709
00:56:27,150 --> 00:56:28,710
Because as we mentioned earlier,

1710
00:56:28,710 --> 00:56:32,410
if you want to enable virtual memory,

1711
00:56:32,410 --> 00:56:36,550
the CPU must support which privilege level?

1712
00:56:36,550 --> 00:56:39,550
It must support that S privilege level.

1713
00:56:39,550 --> 00:56:39,730
Right?

1714
00:56:39,730 --> 00:56:43,030
So it imposes higher demands on the CPU design.

1715
00:56:43,030 --> 00:56:45,690
But if I have some simple CPUs

1716
00:56:45,690 --> 00:56:48,170
used in a relatively simple scenario,

1717
00:56:48,170 --> 00:56:50,730
and I don't need to implement virtual addresses,

1718
00:56:50,730 --> 00:56:54,310
then you don't need to implement that S mode.

1719
00:56:54,310 --> 00:56:55,490
But,

1720
00:56:55,490 --> 00:56:59,170
suppose I also want to, in this simple scenario,

1721
00:56:59,170 --> 00:57:01,930
if I can also provide some protection for memory,

1722
00:57:01,930 --> 00:57:03,970
then I can adopt this approach.

1723
00:57:03,970 --> 00:57:06,450
It's called Physical Memory Protection, or PMP.

1724
00:57:06,450 --> 00:57:08,270
Physical Memory Protection,

1725
00:57:08,270 --> 00:57:09,250
is relatively simple.

1726
00:57:09,250 --> 00:57:14,090
It actually doesn't protect memory using virtualization.

1727
00:57:14,090 --> 00:57:15,250
It's actually very simple.

1728
00:57:15,250 --> 00:57:16,510
That is, you can specify,

1729
00:57:16,510 --> 00:57:21,430
for example, say this computer has a chunk of memory.

1730
00:57:21,430 --> 00:57:25,010
Suppose this chunk of memory is this large,

1731
00:57:25,010 --> 00:57:27,470
say 64K.

1732
00:57:27,470 --> 00:57:28,930
Then for this 64K,

1733
00:57:28,930 --> 00:57:32,230
I can divide up this 64K of memory.

1734
00:57:32,230 --> 00:57:34,890
For instance, this region,

1735
00:57:34,890 --> 00:57:36,330
I'll call it Xon.

1736
00:57:36,330 --> 00:57:38,190
Xon means execute-only.

1737
00:57:38,190 --> 00:57:39,970
That is, I can put in here,

1738
00:57:39,970 --> 00:57:40,370
put what?

1739
00:57:40,370 --> 00:57:41,830
Put instructions.

1740
00:57:41,830 --> 00:57:43,850
By putting instructions,

1741
00:57:43,850 --> 00:57:45,610
that is, the content in here

1742
00:57:45,610 --> 00:57:48,170
I can load it into the CPU and execute it

1743
00:57:48,170 --> 00:57:49,070
Do you understand what I mean by that?

1744
00:57:49,070 --> 00:57:50,950
This is called executable

1745
00:57:50,950 --> 00:57:52,510
Now what about RWX?

1746
00:57:52,510 --> 00:57:53,910
It means the content in here

1747
00:57:53,910 --> 00:57:56,350
can be read, written, and executed

1748
00:57:56,350 --> 00:57:58,790
Now what about this lock?

1749
00:57:58,790 --> 00:57:59,350
It means locked

1750
00:57:59,350 --> 00:58:02,050
which effectively means it can no longer be used

1751
00:58:02,050 --> 00:58:04,350
So in practice, that means

1752
00:58:04,350 --> 00:58:07,350
In our programs, we often talk about what?

1753
00:58:07,350 --> 00:58:09,050
read-only and writable

1754
00:58:09,050 --> 00:58:10,190
This kind of access protection

1755
00:58:10,190 --> 00:58:12,170
can be implemented at this level

1756
00:58:12,170 --> 00:58:13,630
But under this approach,

1757
00:58:13,630 --> 00:58:16,010
we are actually still accessing memory through physical addresses

1758
00:58:16,010 --> 00:58:18,310
but it can already provide a certain level of memory protection

1759
00:58:18,310 --> 00:58:19,270
Do you all understand what I mean?

1760
00:58:19,270 --> 00:58:22,470
This is a simple form of memory management and protection

1761
00:58:22,470 --> 00:58:25,030
So what about the more advanced approach?

1762
00:58:25,030 --> 00:58:27,090
Of course, that means enabling virtual memory.

1763
00:58:27,090 --> 00:58:28,370
The concept of virtual memory,

1764
00:58:28,370 --> 00:58:31,050
I won't say much about it here,

1765
00:58:31,050 --> 00:58:33,930
because it's not a focus of our course.

1766
00:58:33,930 --> 00:58:35,230
In fact,

1767
00:58:35,230 --> 00:58:38,590
if any of you have studied Linux,

1768
00:58:38,590 --> 00:58:41,130
we know that an advanced operating system like Linux,

1769
00:58:41,130 --> 00:58:42,930
it certainly has to implement virtual memory.

1770
00:58:42,930 --> 00:58:45,510
And the so-called concept of virtual memory,

1771
00:58:45,510 --> 00:58:47,510
is that in your program,

1772
00:58:47,510 --> 00:58:49,510
the addresses you access,

1773
00:58:49,510 --> 00:58:51,850
are no longer physical addresses,

1774
00:58:51,850 --> 00:58:52,330
but what?

1775
00:58:52,330 --> 00:58:54,150
But virtual addresses.

1776
00:58:54,150 --> 00:58:57,070
After you've studied Linux,

1777
00:58:57,070 --> 00:58:59,970
this concept should be fairly clear to you.

1778
00:58:59,970 --> 00:59:00,790
That is to say,

1779
00:59:00,790 --> 00:59:02,390
when it runs,

1780
00:59:02,390 --> 00:59:04,090
between this virtual address and the physical address,

1781
00:59:04,090 --> 00:59:05,750
Because in the end, the access that gets executed

1782
00:59:05,750 --> 00:59:06,930
is still a physical address.

1783
00:59:06,930 --> 00:59:08,570
So who performs the conversion

1784
00:59:08,570 --> 00:59:09,710
between these two?

1785
00:59:09,710 --> 00:59:11,990
It is not implemented in software,

1786
00:59:11,990 --> 00:59:13,270
but by a special piece of hardware,

1787
00:59:13,270 --> 00:59:14,550
a dedicated module,

1788
00:59:14,550 --> 00:59:15,570
namely the MMU.

1789
00:59:15,570 --> 00:59:17,310
But this MMU

1790
00:59:17,310 --> 00:59:18,530
is built with special hardware mechanisms,

1791
00:59:18,530 --> 00:59:21,070
which cost money to implement.

1792
00:59:21,070 --> 00:59:23,210
So if, from a cost perspective,

1793
00:59:23,210 --> 00:59:24,670
you decide you don't want virtual memory,

1794
00:59:24,670 --> 00:59:26,130
you can simply drop the MMU module,

1795
00:59:26,130 --> 00:59:26,610
remove it entirely.

1796
00:59:26,610 --> 00:59:27,490
And in that case,

1797
00:59:27,490 --> 00:59:29,010
your CPU doesn't need to support

1798
00:59:29,010 --> 00:59:30,970
Supervisor mode either.

1799
00:59:30,970 --> 00:59:32,810
Alright.

1800
00:59:32,810 --> 00:59:35,630
Now, this thing about memory management and protection

1801
00:59:35,630 --> 00:59:39,310
This is also a key point in the spec that must be covered.

1802
00:59:39,310 --> 00:59:40,110
So just get familiar with it.

1803
00:59:40,110 --> 00:59:43,750
And the last thing in our spec,

1804
00:59:43,750 --> 00:59:45,450
we absolutely have to talk about,

1805
00:59:45,450 --> 00:59:48,650
is that whenever you talk about any CPU's instruction set architecture,

1806
00:59:48,650 --> 00:59:50,490
you can't get away from this topic.

1807
00:59:50,490 --> 00:59:52,550
That is, exception and interrupt handling.

1808
00:59:52,550 --> 00:59:53,690
Right?

1809
00:59:53,690 --> 00:59:59,310
If any of you have studied anything related to operating systems,

1810
00:59:59,310 --> 01:00:01,050
exceptions and interrupts are unavoidable.

1811
01:00:01,050 --> 01:00:03,890
So let's briefly touch on it here,

1812
01:00:03,890 --> 01:00:05,910
since I'll go into more detail later,

1813
01:00:05,910 --> 01:00:07,610
introducing exceptions and interrupts on RISC-V.

1814
01:00:07,610 --> 01:00:09,850
Now these two sentences are actually quoted from

1815
01:00:09,850 --> 01:00:12,090
the spec.

1816
01:00:12,090 --> 01:00:13,630
That is, what counts as an exception,

1817
01:00:13,630 --> 01:00:15,330
and what counts as an interrupt — there is a difference.

1818
01:00:15,330 --> 01:00:17,590
What do we say an exception is?

1819
01:00:17,590 --> 01:00:20,350
Let's just look at the figure below.

1820
01:00:20,350 --> 01:00:22,890
What this figure shows is

1821
01:00:22,890 --> 01:00:25,210
The difference between exceptions and interrupts

1822
01:00:25,210 --> 01:00:27,130
So what does an exception mean?

1823
01:00:27,130 --> 01:00:29,150
Let's look at the diagram on the left.

1824
01:00:29,310 --> 01:00:32,470
So let me give you a general idea of what an exception is.

1825
01:00:32,470 --> 01:00:37,570
Basically, over here on the program side, we have a program.

1826
01:00:37,570 --> 01:00:39,290
A program is made up of instructions one after another.

1827
01:00:39,290 --> 01:00:42,590
So our program might execute them one by one.

1828
01:00:42,590 --> 01:00:44,990
For example, when we reach this one,

1829
01:00:44,990 --> 01:00:46,390
Let me erase this diagram for a moment.

1830
01:00:46,390 --> 01:00:49,250
Suppose we reach this instruction,

1831
01:00:49,250 --> 01:00:52,430
that is, when my arrow points to this instruction.

1832
01:00:52,430 --> 01:00:55,010
For example, say this instruction does something like,

1833
01:00:55,010 --> 01:00:59,250
I go and access memory that cannot be accessed.

1834
01:00:59,250 --> 01:01:01,030
For example, this one.

1835
01:01:01,030 --> 01:01:02,830
This piece of memory is read-only.

1836
01:01:02,830 --> 01:01:04,370
But I insist on writing into it.

1837
01:01:04,370 --> 01:01:05,650
I write something.

1838
01:01:05,650 --> 01:01:06,470
Now, everyone tell me,

1839
01:01:06,470 --> 01:01:08,190
is this legal or not?

1840
01:01:08,190 --> 01:01:09,430
It's definitely illegal.

1841
01:01:09,430 --> 01:01:09,830
So, everyone, think about it this way,

1842
01:01:09,830 --> 01:01:11,270
so this thing,

1843
01:01:11,270 --> 01:01:12,650
once you execute this instruction,

1844
01:01:12,650 --> 01:01:13,390
the CPU

1845
01:01:13,390 --> 01:01:15,030
it definitely knows,

1846
01:01:15,030 --> 01:01:17,370
the CPU will, through some mechanism,

1847
01:01:17,370 --> 01:01:18,090
tell you that

1848
01:01:18,090 --> 01:01:19,930
this instruction of yours is an exception,

1849
01:01:19,930 --> 01:01:20,750
that's actually what it means.

1850
01:01:20,750 --> 01:01:21,750
So,

1851
01:01:21,750 --> 01:01:23,010
it will

1852
01:01:23,010 --> 01:01:25,570
So how does the CPU tell you

1853
01:01:25,570 --> 01:01:27,350
that this instruction caused an exception?

1854
01:01:27,350 --> 01:01:29,470
It will take your current execution flow,

1855
01:01:29,470 --> 01:01:30,350
and stop it,

1856
01:01:30,350 --> 01:01:31,590
right?

1857
01:01:31,590 --> 01:01:32,730
After stopping it,

1858
01:01:32,730 --> 01:01:35,490
it will jump to a special address,

1859
01:01:35,490 --> 01:01:39,830
to execute a special piece of code,

1860
01:01:39,830 --> 01:01:41,150
this piece of execution

1861
01:01:41,150 --> 01:01:42,550
We usually call it an exception handler.

1862
01:01:42,550 --> 01:01:43,630
This piece of code,

1863
01:01:43,630 --> 01:01:44,490
normally,

1864
01:01:44,490 --> 01:01:48,210
is written by yourself.

1865
01:01:48,210 --> 01:01:50,290
What do you want to do about this exception?

1866
01:01:50,290 --> 01:01:51,090
How do you want to handle it?

1867
01:01:51,090 --> 01:01:52,090
You can also choose not to write one.

1868
01:01:52,090 --> 01:01:52,610
If you don't write it,

1869
01:01:52,610 --> 01:01:55,430
then the exception is effectively as if it never happened.

1870
01:01:55,430 --> 01:01:56,730
You get what I mean, right?

1871
01:01:56,730 --> 01:01:57,770
So in general,

1872
01:01:57,770 --> 01:01:59,230
we definitely want to handle the exception.

1873
01:01:59,230 --> 01:02:01,010
And after the handling is done,

1874
01:02:01,010 --> 01:02:07,230
the CPU will go back to the instruction that just caused the exception,

1875
01:02:07,230 --> 01:02:09,270
and run that instruction again.

1876
01:02:09,270 --> 01:02:11,890
This is a very key point about exceptions:

1877
01:02:11,890 --> 01:02:14,430
after the exception handling is finished,

1878
01:02:14,430 --> 01:02:16,890
it will go back to the instruction where the exception

1879
01:02:16,890 --> 01:02:18,350
occurred,

1880
01:02:18,350 --> 01:02:20,290
and execute that instruction again.

1881
01:02:20,290 --> 01:02:24,290
And then it continues to run.

1882
01:02:24,290 --> 01:02:26,730
As for why we do it this way,

1883
01:02:26,730 --> 01:02:28,430
when we talk about exceptions later on,

1884
01:02:28,430 --> 01:02:29,110
I'll explain that to you as well.

1885
01:02:29,110 --> 01:02:30,510
Right now, what we mainly want to do is

1886
01:02:30,510 --> 01:02:32,310
briefly understand the difference between an exception and an interrupt.

1887
01:02:32,310 --> 01:02:34,050
But an interrupt works like this.

1888
01:02:34,050 --> 01:02:35,410
What does an interrupt mean?

1889
01:02:35,410 --> 01:02:36,850
An interrupt means that

1890
01:02:36,850 --> 01:02:40,510
when it is executing this instruction,

1891
01:02:40,510 --> 01:02:42,530
an interrupt might occur.

1892
01:02:42,530 --> 01:02:44,510
Once this interrupt occurs,

1893
01:02:44,510 --> 01:02:46,490
likewise, the CPU will take your

1894
01:02:46,490 --> 01:02:47,890
current execution flow,

1895
01:02:47,890 --> 01:02:48,710
and stop it,

1896
01:02:48,710 --> 01:02:50,370
switching over to execute a

1897
01:02:50,370 --> 01:02:51,750
new interrupt handler.

1898
01:02:51,750 --> 01:02:55,110
Then, after this interrupt handler has finished executing,

1899
01:02:55,110 --> 01:02:56,810
it will also return,

1900
01:02:56,810 --> 01:02:57,650
but when it returns,

1901
01:02:57,650 --> 01:02:59,850
Now notice: does it return like before,

1902
01:02:59,850 --> 01:03:02,150
going back to that earlier instruction and re-executing it?

1903
01:03:02,150 --> 01:03:03,190
No.

1904
01:03:03,190 --> 01:03:06,150
It returns to the instruction after the one

1905
01:03:06,150 --> 01:03:10,040
that was just interrupted, and executes that.

1906
01:03:10,040 --> 01:03:12,120
Now some of you might ask,

1907
01:03:12,120 --> 01:03:15,420
why do we separate these two cases?

1908
01:03:15,420 --> 01:03:17,360
Let me briefly explain.

1909
01:03:17,360 --> 01:03:18,620
What is an exception?

1910
01:03:18,620 --> 01:03:20,820
What does an exception actually mean?

1911
01:03:20,820 --> 01:03:22,040
An exception is essentially,

1912
01:03:22,040 --> 01:03:25,040
a kind of problem triggered by your own program

1913
01:03:25,040 --> 01:03:26,100
something going wrong.

1914
01:03:26,100 --> 01:03:27,720
Something went wrong.

1915
01:03:27,720 --> 01:03:29,380
And the CPU,

1916
01:03:29,380 --> 01:03:29,920
in effect,

1917
01:03:29,920 --> 01:03:31,060
when an exception occurs,

1918
01:03:31,060 --> 01:03:33,480
gives you a chance to correct yourself.

1919
01:03:33,480 --> 01:03:34,660
See what I mean?

1920
01:03:34,660 --> 01:03:36,540
What do I mean by a chance to correct yourself?

1921
01:03:36,540 --> 01:03:38,540
It will interrupt your program

1922
01:03:38,540 --> 01:03:40,480
and make you run a recovery program.

1923
